N2  - The increasing prevalence of caesarean sections has led to a growing incidence of uterine niches and associated caesarean scar disorders. Practitioners are hence faced with the new challenge of identifying and diagnosing uterine niches by imaging approaches. A uterine niche is defined as a myometrial indentation at the site of a caesarean scar that is visible on ultrasound imaging. Hence, in this study, we aimed to apply machine learning algorithms to detect uterine niches. The algorithm was trained to identify sonographic examinations, conducted before and after scar repair surgery at St. Hedwig Clinic (Regensburg), using a multiple-instance learning approach. Raw, unannotated single images exhibiting niches were used for training. Using diverse approaches, we systematically summarised single images into embeddings and used aggregation strategies integrating whole exams from single embeddings. The Sparse Gaussian Process Multiple Instance Learning (SGPMIL) framework combined with an EfficientNetV2 backbone demonstrated the strongest predictive performance (area under the curve (AUC): 0.89, accuracy: 81 %, F1-score: 0.80). To improve interpretability in addition to the classification by SGPMIL, Grad-CAM visualisations partially succeed to highlight regions within the ultrasound images that were relevant to diagnosis. Our findings demonstrate the feasibility of machine learning–based diagnostic support in the early diagnosis of uterine niches to facilitate diagnostic decision-making. Moreover, they highlight promising methodological approaches for sonographic image analysis in addressing other diagnostic challenges in the context of niche detection.
