N2  - Background: With the rise of cesarean section (CS) deliveries, the incidence of uterine niches, exacerbated fibrosis forming myometrial indentations, has also increased. Their co-occurrence with poor reproductive health is collectively recognized as cesarean scar disorder (CSD). Here, we aimed to shed light on the association of CSD severity and the transcriptomic and histological features of uterine niches. Methods: Niche samples were collected from uterine scar repair surgeries. Bulk-RNA sequencing served to define the transcriptome profiles among 19 samples. Imaging methods were used to explore relevant scar features in a library of 32 samples. Results: Severity of CSD symptoms shaped the principal component analyses of transcriptomic profiles. Hence, patients with one symptom were compared to those with more. Greater transcriptomic heterogeneity was observed in women with more severe CSD manifestations. In particular, genes linked to inflammation, including neutrophil chemoattractants (CXCL1, CXCL3, CXCL5, CXCL8), and monocyte/macrophage chemoattractant CXCL17, were upregulated, as were fibrosis-associated genes (SPP1, MMP7, MMP11, IL-11, ITGB6). Pathway analyses indicated NK-cell activation. Intriguingly, gene sets related to ciliated cells in the uterus, accounting for endometrial epithelium and glands, were upregulated in samples from women with more severe symptomatology, along with PTGS2 and LIF, related to inflammation and endometrial receptivity. In line with this, histologically, scar tissues with greater intertwining of CD10+ endometrial glands (associated with cycles of growth and apoptosis) exhibited greater fibrosis and complex ectopic structures, including fat tissue and cysts. The CD10+ endometrial tissue in the scar gathered CD68+ macrophages, many co-expressing CD206, with the exception of the tissue surrounding cysts, which accumulated only CD68+CD206- macrophages. Conclusions: The scar tissue exhibits a complex spatial architecture characterized by endometrial gland integration, fibrosis, and immune cell infiltration. CSD severity is associated with distinct inflammatory, fibrotic, and endometrial-related transcriptomic changes, suggesting a graded molecular and structural remodeling underlying symptom burden.
