N2  - Background: Whilst anatomical abnormalities and hormonal imbalances are main causes for infertility in women, alterations in the maternal immune system, including the complement system, may contribute to the 16% of cases classified as unexplained. Semi-allogenic fetal cells require a highly regulated, yet dynamic and tolerance-inducing immune response, that allows the fetus to properly implant (pro-inflammatory features) and grow (anti-inflammatory features) in the uterine environment. Blastocyst invasion is accompanied by tissue remodeling, including re-organization of ECM and neo-angiogenesis, that also allows for the establishment of a specialized niche for leukocytes. The main immune cell type in the pregnant and non-pregnant uterus, NK cells, both shape and are shaped by these processes. Mice with a global deletion of C5aR2 show fetal resorptions, accompanied by alterations in the NK cell compartment, already early in pregnancy. Methods: worked into the results part Results: Bulk-proteomics of uteri from C5aR2-deficient females showed differentially expressed proteins only in the non-pregnant state. Further, pathway enrichment analysis revealed several altered signaling cascades, including ECM and Actin-skeleton organization. Histological and fluorescent staining of cryo-sections from uteri reexamined alterations in tissue constitution in the non-pregnant state. Spatial Transcriptomics experiments localized differentially expressed proteins/genes of the above-mentioned pathways in regions that undergo fundamental remodeling in early pregnancy. High-dimensional flow cytometry of the NK cell compartment of C5aR2-deficient mice revealed retention of tissue marker CD49aexpressing NK cells in the circulation, as well as a more terminally matured and exhausted phenotype of uterine NK cells in the non-pregnant state. Conclusions: Hence, the complement-receptor C5aR2 seems to not only have an influence on the leukocyte department directly but also shapes the uterine environment. Deletion-induced alterations of tissue remodeling may be causative for a modified niche for NK cells, shaping their phenotype, and lead to failure of proper blastocyst implantation.
