N2  - Most causes for infertility in women can be attributed to anatomical abnormalities and hormonal imbalances. Since the maternal immune response against the semi-allogenic fetus requires strict regulations and dynamic changes between pro- and anti-inflammatory states throughout pregnancy, an altered immune system may explain the remaining 16%. Moreover, the invasion of the blastocyst into tissue is characterized by tissue remodeling with breakdown and neo-synthesis of ECM, neoangiogenesis and immunological tolerance against foreign antigens. In pregnancy, uterine NK cells both influence and are influenced by these mechanisms. Mice with a global deletion of C5aR2 show decreased litter sizes, most likely introduced already early in pregnancy, since higher numbers of fetal resorptions were observed within the first trimester. Differential expression analysis of a bulk-proteomics dataset from uteri of C5aR2-deficient in comparison with WT mice revealed alterations only in the non-pregnant state. Pathway enrichment analysis of these proteins indicated alterations in ECM and Actin-skeleton organization that was reexamined through Masson-Goldner-Trichrome and fluorescent staining. Spatial Transcriptomics helped mapping those pathways within the uterus to regions that undergo fundamental remodeling in early pregnancy. Previous data indicated alterations in the NK cell compartment that was deciphered in more detail with the help of high-dimensional flow cytometry. In non-pregnant C5ar2–/– mice, NK cells expressing the tissue marker CD49a were retained in the circulation and uterine NK cells showed a more terminally matured and exhausted phenotype. Together, these data suggest a non-classical role of C5aR2, influencing not only the leukocyte compartment, but also tissue integrity of the murine uterus. Deletion of C5aR2 may lead to reduced tissue remodeling that is pivotal for proper blastocyst-implantation but also provides an immunological niche that allows for leukocyte recruitment and maturation. Since the uterine environment is a highly complex network of non-immune and immune-cell types, causative relations remain to be elucidated.
