N2  - Background: A uterine isthmocele, also referred to as a cesarean scar defect or niche, is an increasingly recognized complication of prior cesarean delivery that may lead to abnormal uterine bleeding, pelvic pain, infertility, and adverse obstetric outcomes1-2. Traditional surgical management includes hysteroscopic and laparoscopic repair; however, intraoperative visualization of the defect and complete excision of fibrotic tissue can be challenging3. In many cases, laparoscopic repair relies on simultaneous hysteroscopic assistance for accurate localization of the niche, a process that often requires a second surgeon or assistant. Recent advances in minimally invasive gynecologic surgery, particularly the integration of robotic platforms with intraoperative ultrasound guidance, provide a single-surgeon solution. This approach enhances precision and depth assessment, improves safety, and allows the operating surgeon to maintain real-time visualization and control from the robotic console while directly guiding the repair. Objective: The purpose of this case report is to describe a novel minimally invasive technique utilizing robotic intraoperative ultrasound guidance for the repair of a symptomatic uterine isthmocele. Materials and Methods: A 39-year-old G10P0262 with a history of two prior cesarean deliveries, a cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy managed with dilation and curettage, recurrent miscarriage, and hysteroscopic adhesiolysis presented with abnormal uterine bleeding and secondary infertility. Saline infusion sonohysterography demonstrated a cesarean scar defect with 1 mm of residual myometrial thickness. The patient underwent hysteroscopy and robotic-assisted laparoscopic isthmocele repair. Intraoperative robotic ultrasound was utilized for precise localization of the niche, confirmed hysteroscopically (Figure 1). Fibrotic tissue was excised to healthy myometrium, and the defect was closed in two layers under continuous ultrasound guidance to ensure adequate myometrial thickness and restoration of the uterine contour. The postoperative course was uncomplicated. Follow-up sonohysterography performed two months after surgery demonstrated restoration of a 6–7 mm myometrial thickness at the repair site. The patient subsequently conceived spontaneously and was 15 weeks pregnant at the time of follow-up, confirming restoration of fertility. [Formula presented] Results: Robotic ultrasound-guided repair resulted in normalization of myometrial thickness, resolution of abnormal bleeding, and spontaneous conception within six months of surgery. Conclusions: Intraoperative robotic ultrasound guidance represents a safe and effective minimally invasive approach for the management of symptomatic uterine isthmocele. This technique allows for precise localization, complete excision of fibrotic tissue, and accurate multilayer closure under direct visualization, all achievable by a single surgeon without the need for concurrent hysteroscopic assistance. Robotic ultrasound-guided repair may be particularly advantageous in centers with limited surgical staff or in cases requiring enhanced depth assessment and tissue differentiation. Support: No financial support to disclose. REFERENCES: 1. Tsuji S, Nobuta Y, Hanada T, et al. Prevalence, definition, and etiology of cesarean scar defect and treatment of cesarean scar disorder: A narrative review. Reprod Med Biol. 2023;22(1):e12532. Published 2023 Aug 9. doi:10.1002/rmb2.12532 2. Tower AM, Frishman GN. Cesarean scar defects: an underrecognized cause of abnormal uterine bleeding and other gynecologic complications. J Minim Invasive Gynecol. 2013;20(5):562-572. doi:10.1016/j.jmig.2013.03.008 3. Ramezani Z, Goodarzi S, Rashidian P, et al. A comparison between laparoscopy and hysteroscopy approaches in the treatment of symptomatic isthmocele: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Eur J Obstet Gynecol Reprod Biol X. 2025;27:100405. Published 2025 Jun 4. doi:10.1016/j.eurox.2025.100405
