N2  - Background: Gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD) is a term including spectrum of hydatidiform mole (complete and partial), invasive mole, choriocarcinoma and placental-site trophoblastic tumour. It as an abnormal proliferation of trophoblastic tissue associated with pregnancy and have chances to evolving to malignancy. A caesarean scar pregnancy is an ectopic pregnancy in which the gestational sac is seen within the myometrium of a previous cesarean scar. Its incidence is 1:2,216, with a rate of 6.1% in women with ectopic pregnancies and at least one previous cesarean section. It is a very rare condition that the GTD emerging from a cesarean scar. Case: Patient 35 years old with history of curettage due to anembryonic pregnancy 1 month prior, said to have placental residue. She had history of 2 cesarean section on 2016 and 2019 Later the ultrasound revealed irregular snow-storm like mass sized 52 x 63 x 64 mm penetrated 18 mm into the niche. Seen irregularity on anterior myometrium with vascular growth. MRI showed heterogenous lobulated solid mass suggested malignant mass arising into uterus, bilateral parametrium and right ovary with parailiac multiple lymphadenopathy. Conclusion: This case highlights a rare occurrence of Gestational Trophoblastic Disease (GTD) developing from a cesarean scar pregnancy, which is an unusual form of ectopic pregnancy. Early detection and appropriate management are crucial for better prognosis and patient outcomes. Due to its rarity, this case adds valuable insight to the limited literature on cesarean scar-related GTD.
