N2  - Purpose: To describe a combined hysteroscopic and robot-assisted laparoscopic technique for the repair of a large grade 3 isthmocele in a patient with absent residual myometrial thickness.Methods: A 37-year-old woman with three previous caesarean deliveries, abnormal uterine bleeding and secondary infertility underwent imaging demonstrating an 8 × 7 × 6 cm isthmocele with no measurable residual myometrial thickness. A combined minimally invasive technique was performed including hysteroscopic transillumination, indocyanine green (ICG)–guided fluorescence using the Firefly® system, cold excision of fibrotic tissue and two-layer laparoscopic reconstruction.Results: Hysteroscopic transillumination and ICG fluorescence enabled precise differentiation between healthy myometrium and fibrotic scar tissue, allowing safe excision. The procedure was completed without intraoperative or postoperative complications. At six-week follow-up, transvaginal ultrasound confirmed complete anatomical restoration and symptom resolution.Conclusion: The combined hysteroscopic–robotic approach with real-time ICG fluorescence enhances surgical accuracy and represents a safe and effective minimally invasive option for complex large-volume isthmocele repair.
