N2  - Introduction: Cesarean scar pregnancy is among the rarest forms of ectopic pregnancy. It occurs when the pregnancy implants on or within the niche of a previous cesarean scar. The objective of this study was to describe the diagnostic Methods for cesarean scar pregnancy and the various therapeutic approaches. Methods: This was a descriptive retrospective cross-sectional in the "A" Gynecology and Obstetrics department at Charles Nicolle Hospital Tunis. It spanned an eight-year period from January 2016 to January 2023 focusing on cases of cesarean scar pregnancies. Results: We collected 30 cases of cesarean scar pregnancy.The average age of patients in our study was 36 years. Diagnosis was primarily based on suprapubic and endovaginal ultrasound augmented by color Doppler. Therapeutic management after confirmed diagnosis was as follows: Seven patients (23%) received treatment via intramuscular methotrexate injection. Thirteen patients (43%) underwent ultrasound-guided aspiration. Six patients (20%) were treated with immediate ultrasound-guided aspiration combined with intamuscular methotrexate injection. Two patients (7%) received in situ methotrexate injection under ultrasound guidance. Two patients (7%) were treated with immediate laparotomy for resection. Three patients experienced hemorrhage during treatment. Among the factors associated with a risk of hemorrhaget the following were identified: Gestational age was higher in patients who experienced hemorrhage (p < 0.032). Patients presenting with metrorrhagia at initial examination had a higher risk of developing hemorrhage (p = 0.01). The gestational sac diameter was larger in patients who experienced hemorrhage (p = 0.04). Conclusion: The early diagnosisof cesarean scar pregnancy is crucial to guide optimal management.
