N2  - OBJECTIVE: To investigate whether patients who have undergone uterus transplantation (UTx) are at high risk of isthmocele formation following one or more cesarean deliveries. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Data was collected retrospectively from the largest living donor UTx program (Baylor University Medical Center) and the largest deceased donor UTx program (Cleveland Clinic). Both centers have seen a rise in patient interest in using the same donor uterus for multiple pregnancies. Each delivery following UTx is performed via cesarean section. Given that UTx recipients require immunosuppressive therapy to prevent graft rejection, we hypothesize that immunosuppression and chronic rejection may impair wound healing and contribute to increased postpartum isthmocele formation. Maintenance immunosuppression regimens during pregnancy consisted of tacrolimus and prednisone. Recipients of UTx who underwent cesarean section without graft hysterectomy at time of delivery were included in the final analysis. The primary outcome was the presence of a visible cesarean scar niche, or isthmocele, on a postpartum transvaginal ultrasound. Analysis of ultrasound images were performed by three independent reviewers to characterize the isthmocele by residual myometrial thickness, distance from external cervical os, and depth of niche. Patient and donor characteristics including BMI, age, donor parity, number of graft rejection episodes, donor prior cesarean sections, pregnancy complications (e.g., gestational diabetes, pre-eclampsia, premature rupture of membranes, pre-term labor), and time from uterine transplant to live birth were compared. RESULTS: A total of 43 UTx recipients were screened and nineteen met inclusion criteria. Four patients were identified by our team’s independent reviewers as having an isthmocele. Recipients and donors had median ages of 32 and 33 years, and median BMIs of 24 and 28 kg/m2, respectively. Seventeen of nineteen eligible donors had a prior cesarean delivery, including one with two cesarean deliveries. The average time from uterus transplant to first cesarean section was 428 days (range: 392, 1430). There was no correlation between pregnancy complications in transplant recipients and the subsequent identification of a cesarean scar defect on ultrasound. CONCLUSIONS: In this pilot study involving a small cohort of UTx recipients who retained their donor uterus after at least one cesarean delivery, the incidence of isthmocele formation (21%) was not higher than what has been reported in the literature previously for non-transplant populations (24-84%). Further studies are needed to validate these findings. Prospective research should also aim to clarify the incidence, risk factors, and clinical implications of cesarean scar defects in immunosuppressed patients to support counseling of prospective UTx recipients. IMPACT STATEMENT: As uterus transplantation becomes more accessible for uterine-factor infertility, addressing complications such as isthmocele will be essential to optimize maternal and graft outcomes.
