N2  - Objective This consensus statement aims to guide clinicians in the diagnosis, management and prevention of cesarean scar niche. Target Population Patients undergoing cesarean delivery or patients with cesarean scar disorder. Options Options for management include expectant, medical and surgical management. These will depend on symptoms, residual myometrial thickness, and future fertility plans. Outcomes Outcomes include resolution of symptoms, repair of defect and subsequent successful pregnancy. Benefits, Harms, and Costs This guideline aims to benefit patients undergoing cesarean delivery or those suffering from cesarean scar disorder and to offer healthcare providers an evidence-based approach to the prevention, diagnosis and management of cesarean scar defects. Evidence Published clinical trials, randomized controlled trials, observational studies, population studies, and systematic review articles indexed in PubMed and the Cochrane database were identified between January 1st, 2005 and April 30th, 2025 using the search terms “cesarean scar niche”, “cesarean scar defect”, and “isthmocele”. Results were restricted to studies written in English. Validation Methods The authors rated the quality of evidence and strength of recommendations using the Grading of Recommendations Assessment, Development and Evaluation (GRADE) approach. See Appendix A . Intended Audience All health care providers who perform cesarean delivery or who care for patients who have previously undergone a cesarean delivery and may be experiencing symptoms of cesarean scar disorder, including obstetrician gynaecologists, family physicians, registered nurses, nurse practitioners and radiologists. Tweetable Abstract Cesarean scar niche is a common consequence of cesarean delivery. Symptomatic patients suffering from cesarean scar disorder can be treated expectantly or with medical or surgical options. Asymptomatic patients do not require treatment. SUMMARY STATEMENTS 1. Cesarean scar niches are common in patients who have had a cesarean delivery, with most being asymptomatic (high), but there are few studies that allow for a comprehensive understanding of the epidemiology of this common problem (high). 2. Cesarean scar niche is defined as a myometrial indentation at the site of a previous cesarean hysterotomy, with a depth of at least 2 mm, as measured by transvaginal ultrasound at least 3 months following cesarean delivery (moderate). 3. Abnormal uterine bleeding, specifically prolonged menstrual duration and early cycle inter-menstrual bleeding (post-menstrual spotting) is a commonly reported symptom in those with symptomatic niche (high). Pelvic pain and secondary unexplained infertility are also described symptoms related to symptomatic niches (high). 4. Hormonal menstrual suppression, particularly the levonorgestrel intrauterine device, has been shown to improve niche-associated abnormal uterine bleeding and pelvic pain symptoms (high). 5. Hysteroscopic niche remodelling is a safe surgical approach when the residual myometrial thickness is ≥3mm to improve niche-associated abnormal uterine bleeding and otherwise unexplained secondary infertility (moderate). 6. A laparoscopic or vaginal approach to niche revision can be used when the residual myometrial thickness is <3mm and can improve abnormal uterine bleeding symptoms (moderate). There are few data to support this approach to treat infertility or to improve outcomes with assisted reproductive technologies (ART) (low). 7. Current data do not support surgical management of cesarean scar niche to improve pregnancy outcomes (moderate). 8. Performing an incision above the vesicouterine junction for patients undergoing cesarean delivery in the active phase of labour may decrease the risk of cesarean scar niche (moderate). 9. In patients undergoing elective cesarean delivery, an unlocked two-layer closure that does not include the endometrium, is associated with a lower risk of cesarean scar niche (moderate). RECOMMENDATIONS 1) Clinicians should use transvaginal ultrasound, with or without gel or saline infusion, as the preferred imaging modality for diagnosing cesarean scar niche (strong, high). 2) The following ultrasonographic niche measurements should be reported at minimum: niche depth, length, width, residual myometrial thickness and adjacent myometrial thickness (strong, high). 3) Providers should become familiar with the diagnostic criteria for Cesarean Scar Disorder and consider this diagnosis in symptomatic individuals following cesarean delivery with an ultrasound diagnosis of a niche (strong, high). 4) Since asymptomatic patients with a cesarean scar niche do not require treatment, and since the monitoring of pregnancy and childbirth should not be influenced by the presence of a niche, screening ultrasounds for a niche in patients with a prior cesarean is unnecessary and not recommended (strong, high). 5) In symptomatic patients who do not desire fertility, medical menstrual suppression with combined hormonal contraceptives or the levonorgestrel intrauterine device should be offered (strong, high). 6) Surgical management may be considered for symptomatic patients who do not tolerate or respond to conservative measures (conditional, moderate). 7) Hysteroscopic niche remodelling may be considered in symptomatic patients when the residual myometrial thickness is ≥3mm (conditional, moderate). 8) Symptomatic patients who desire fertility may be considered for surgical niche repair (laparoscopic repair with hysteroscopic guidance or vaginal repair), especially when the residual myometrial thickness is <3mm (conditional, moderate). 9) Comprehensive counselling should be provided to all patients with cesarean scar disorder, outlining the potential risks, benefits, alternatives, and limitations of treatment, with an emphasis on shared decision making (good practice point). 10) Patients should be counselled that surgical management of cesarean scar niche solely to prevent adverse pregnancy outcomes is not recommended (conditional, low). 11) Patients with cesarean scar niche should be counselled about future risk of cesarean scar pregnancy and placenta accreta spectrum disorder and should be encouraged to obtain an early pregnancy ultrasound in subsequent pregnancies. Otherwise, monitoring of the pregnancy and delivery should not be influenced by the presence of a niche diagnosed before pregnancy (good practice point). 12) In patients undergoing cesarean delivery in labour with advanced cervical dilatation (≥5cm), providers should consider performing the hysterotomy incision above the vesicouterine junction to reduce the risk of niche formation and its associated consequences (conditional, moderate). 13) In patients undergoing elective cesarean delivery or cesarean in early labor, providers should consider closing the hysterotomy in an unlocked double-layer fashion, where the first layer does not incorporate the endometrium. This technique has been associated with a reduced risk of CSN and its sequelae (conditional, moderate).
