N2  - Study Objective: To demonstrate the distorted anatomy in the vesicouterine region that can be caused by important isthmocele. Also, highlight the aid of indocyanine green for its identification and delimitation in order to perform a precise and safe laparoscopic hysterectomy. Design: Case report illustrated with video. Setting: Patient under general anesthesia, placed in lithotomy position, with arms alongside the body and legs 80 degrees abducted in adjustable stirrups. One conventional laparoscopic portal was positioned in the iliac fossa, and three robotic portals: one 10mm umbilical and two 5mm on the right and left flanks. The cervix was manipulated with a disposable uterine manipulator. Indocyanine green was injected into the ureters via cystoscopy and into the isthmocele via hysteroscopy Patients or Participants: Female pacient, 51 years old, no comorbitidies, with 02 previous cesarean sections, and no longer reproductive desire. Complaining of chronic pelvic pain and irregular uterine bleeding outside the menstrual period, no response to oral contraception use. On MRI: uterus vol 156cc with adenomyosis and isthmocele containing a hemorrhagic collection, extending to the left paracervical and vesicouterine regions, causing bladder compression, measuring 4,3x2,3x3,0cm (vol 15ml). Interventions: Laparoscopic hysterectomy using indocyanine green for isthmocele and ureter identification. Measurements and Primary Results: The surgery lasted 2h, with minimal blood loss. The patient had good postoperative evolution, with no pain complaints in the postoperative period, and was discharged after 1 day. Conclusion: In this case, we observed the advantage of laparoscopic surgery for fine dissection of structures, bringing safety to the procedure, as well as we were able to use complementary tools, such as indocyanine green, to optimize and aid in precise dissection.
