N2  - Study Objective: To illustrate the diagnostic evaluation and surgical management of a complex uterine niche. Design: Surgical video. Setting: Operating room. Patients or Participants: A patient with a large, complex uterine niche identified on imaging underwent robotic-assisted laparoscopic niche resection and repair. Interventions: With the increasing incidence of cesarean deliveries, uterine niches are becoming more prevalent. This video highlights a step-by-step approach to robotic-assisted niche resection and repair, emphasizing the following key principles: • Integration of transvaginal ultrasound and MRI to optimize preoperative planning. • Adequate bladder mobilization, ensuring at least 2 cm of separation from the niche site. • Use of intraoperative hysteroscopy, indocyanine green (ICG), or methylene blue to delineate niche borders and confirm complete closure. • Minimal use of electrosurgical energy during resection to promote optimal healing. • Administration of vasopressin to achieve hemostasis. • In cases of significant uterine retroflexion, round ligament plication was performed to reduce tension at the repair site. Measurements and Primary Results: Not applicable. Conclusion: This surgical video demonstrates a comprehensive approach to the evaluation and management of a complex uterine niche. It highlights the use of multimodal imaging, including transvaginal ultrasound and MRI, for surgical planning, and showcases the combined use of hysteroscopy, robotic-assisted laparoscopy with Firefly mode, and round ligament plication to facilitate precise resection and promote optimal wound healing.
