N2  - Study Objective: To highlight a case of a cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy and demonstrate surgical principles important for safe resection Design: Surgical video case presentation Setting: Tertiary care academic center Patients or Participants: This is a video case presentation of a 38 year old G6P2032 patient who presented for confirmation of pregnancy. Transvaginal ultrasonography showed hypervascular and irregularly shaped gestational sac at the level of the patient's previous cesarean scar and a fetal pole measuring 10w2d with fetal heart tones. There was no residual myometrial tissue between the uterus and the bladder. The patient desired future fertility and the decision was made to proceed with robotic-assisted cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy resection and hysteroscopic-guided myometrial repair. Interventions: Robotic-assisted excision of cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy and hysteroscopic-guided myometrial repair Measurements and Primary Results: Surgical techniques demonstrated in this video include: identification of normal anatomic landmarks using careful dissection and retrograde bladder filling, proactive prevention of blood loss with mechanical and medical strategies, and multilayer myometrial reconstruction with hysteroscopic guidance. The patient did clinically well postoperatively and beta-HCG quantitative levels were trended to <5. Postoperative imaging revealed a myometrial thickness of 1 cm and no evidence of isthmocele. Conclusion: Cesarean scar ectopic pregnancies can be effectively managed with robotic-assisted resection and subsequent myometrial repair.
