N2  - Study Objective: To demonstrate the hysteroscopic repair of a cesarean scar defect (isthmocele) in a retroflexed uterus in a patient with recurrent failed embryo transfers, and to highlight the role of surgical correction in restoring uterine receptivity and achieving successful pregnancy. Design: This is a case presentation and surgical video demonstrating operative technique. Postoperative follow-up extended through subsequent embryo transfer and confirmation of clinical pregnancy. Setting: Procedures were performed in an ambulatory surgery center operating room with the patient positioned in dorsal lithotomy. A standard hysteroscopic setup was used, ensuring ergonomic surgeon posture and continuous visualization. Patients or Participants: A single patient with secondary infertility following four failed embryo transfers was selected. The patient was diagnosed with isthmocele associated with a retroflexed uterus. Consent for surgical intervention and use of deidentified clinical data for educational purposes was obtained. Interventions: Hysteroscopic resection of the isthmocele was performed using monopolar energy to excise the fibrotic edges and create a smooth myometrial contour, thereby facilitating fluid drainage and restoring endometrial integrity. Measurements and Primary Results: Preoperative diagnosis was confirmed with transvaginal sonography demonstrating a hypoechoic cesarean scar defect with persistent intracavitary fluid accumulation. Postoperative imaging demonstrated resolution of fluid stasis. The patient underwent frozen embryo transfer following a two-month healing interval and achieved a successful clinical pregnancy on the first attempt. Conclusion: Hysteroscopic repair of isthmocele in the setting of a retroflexed uterus can improve uterine environment and facilitate successful implantation after repeated IVF failure. Early recognition and surgical correction of cesarean scar defects should be considered in the infertility workup, particularly when fluid accumulation is present. Further studies are warranted to standardize patient selection and optimize timing of intervention relative to assisted reproductive treatments.
