N2  - Study Objective: This video review aims to demonstrate the robotic surgical approach used to repair a complex cervical defect with isthmocele repair techniques and placement of abdominal cerclage for preconception management Design: Single patient, surgical video review, outpatient post-operative follow-up for 3 months Setting: Gynecologic operating room with Da Vinci robotic operating system set-up, patient positioned in lithotomy position Patients or Participants: Patient was selected due to complicated cervical defect identified on imaging following preconception counseling with REI Interventions: Robotic assisted surgical approach to perform a wide excision of cervical defect followed by multi-layer closure of the excision. Trans-abdominal cerclage was then placed with Mersilene to ensure cervical integrity. Hysteroscopy and cystoscopy were performed intra-operatively to ensure adequate repair of the defect, patency of the cervical canal and uterine cavity, and to ensure no injury to the bladder. Measurements and Primary Results: Intraoperative assessments were utilized to evaluate the isthmocele excision and repair. These intraoperative findings indicate success of the repair based on robotic and hysteroscopic assessments on the integrity of the cervical canal and uterine cavity. Post-operative follow-up appointments have indicated that the patient is doing well. Continued follow up and imaging is on-going to assess the integrity of the repair and success of future pregnancy Conclusion: This video highlights the application of robotic assisted isthmocele repair techniques for management of complex cervical defects. It also demonstrates that trans-abdominal cerclage is a technique that can be applied to ensure cervical integrity after the repair of a complex cervical defect. Further studies with larger cohorts are warranted to determine the broader applicability of these techniques and to establish standardized protocols for their application.
