N2  - Study Objective: To demonstrate a combined laparoscopic and hysteroscopic approach for isthmocele repair using the Jack O’ Lantern technique. Design: Case report Setting: Operating room in a private hospital in Mexico City. Patients or Participants: 39-year-old woman, gravida 3, with one abortion and one cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy. She presented with abnormal uterine bleeding, pelvic heaviness, and ultrasound findings of a symptomatic isthmocele with only 2 mm of residual myometrial thickness. Surgical correction was indicated due to her reproductive plans and severity of symptoms. Interventions: Combined laparoscopic and hysteroscopic isthmocele repair using the Jack O’ Lantern technique. Measurements and Primary Results: The patient was taken to the operating room and underwent a combined laparoscopic-hysteroscopic procedure. The hysteroscopy confirmed the presence of a wide isthmocele with fluid accumulation. A uterine manipulator and bladder catheter were placed. Laparoscopically, the vesicouterine peritoneum was dissected, and the bladder was pushed down to fully expose the defect. The isthmocele borders were excised to healthy tissue, followed by a layered closure of the myometrium using barbed sutures. The hysteroscope was used intermittently to transilluminate and delineate the borders of the niche (“Jack O’ Lantern sign”), aiding in precise identification and suturing of the defect. The patient tolerated the procedure well, with minimal bleeding and no intraoperative complications. She was discharged the following day. Conclusion: Combined laparoscopic and hysteroscopic repair of isthmoceles provides superior visualization and anatomical restoration. The Jack O’ Lantern technique, which utilizes hysteroscopic light for laparoscopic guidance, enhances surgical precision and improves patient outcomes.
