N2  - Study Objective: To review the sonographic correlates, clinical significance, and surgical technique in isthmoceles repair, with a focus on implications for infertility, obstetric morbidity, and cesarean scar ectopic pregnancies. Design: Narrative review of published literature combined with video-based demonstration of two robotic surgical techniques for isthmocele repair in an IVF/egg retrieval patient and a cesarean scar ectopic. Setting: Procedures were performed in an tertiary academic center with a multi-port robotic approach. Patients or Participants: Two patients were selected for case demonstration: one undergoing in vitro fertilization with secondary infertility and the second with a cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy at 8 weeks gestation. Interventions: Robotic-assisted laparoscopic excision of the isthmoceles were performed in both cases. The procedure included hemostatic control (uterine ligation, vasopressin, uterine artery embolization, and surgical technique) as well as niche identificaiton, excision and repair technique. Measurements and Primary Results: Preoperative and intraoperative findings are presented. A review of key literature for patient selection and pertinent clinical correlates are reviewed related to obstetric and infertility outcomes. Conclusion: Isthmocele is a clinically prevalent and significant sequaelae of prior cesarean delivery with potential impacts on fertility, implantation, and obstetric outcomes. Robotic-assisted repair offers a precise and fertility-sparing approach, particularly for patients undergoing IVF or presenting with cesarean scar ectopics. This video abstract reviews key literature and surgical technique in isthmocele repairs.
