N2  - Study Objective: To demonstrate minimally invasive management of two rare cesarean scar complications: uterine body cesarean scar defect (CSD) and early c-section scar ectopic. Design: CSD, typically located in isthmus, upper endocervical canal, is a known cause of abnormal uterine bleeding. This case involved an uncommonly located defect within the uterine body, extending through the anterior myometrium and communicating with the endometrial cavity. Its complex location required hysteroscopic-guided robotic excision with multilayer myometrial reconstruction. The second case involved a cesarean scar pregnancy (CSP), a rare implantation at a prior uterine incision. Early first-trimester diagnosis allowed fertility-preserving hysteroscopic removal before deep myometrial invasion. Setting: Academic tertiary referral center. Patients or Participants: 38-year-old G3P0020 with two prior C-sections, abnormal bleeding, and anterior uterine body scar defect. 25-year-old G8P4124 with four prior C-sections, early pregnancy, and CSP on transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS). Interventions: Case 1: Saline sonogram showed anterior uterine body scar. MRI showed fibrosis with anterior wall thinning. Hysteroscopic-guided ICG-NIRF mapping confirmed an oblique 3×1 cm scar in the uterine body. Robotic excision and layered closure were completed. Case 2: TVUS showed a first-trimester CSP with > 3 mm myometrial thickness. Operative hysteroscopy with cold loop resection and hydrodissection removed the ectopic tissue; the isthmocele was also resected to reduce recurrence risk. Measurements and Primary Results: Case 1: Postoperative recovery was uneventful with resolution of symptoms. Histology showed benign myometrium with fibrosis. Despite challenging anatomy, ICG and NIRF-aided localization, and robotic repair enabled precise, layered closure, preserving fertility. Case 2: β-hCG declined post-op; pathology confirmed chorionic villi. Patient remains asymptomatic. Hysteroscopy with hydrodissection proved effective for fertility-sparing CSP management. Conclusion: Minimally invasive management of complex cesarean scar sequelae demands individualized, anatomy-guided strategies. Uterine body defects are an underrecognized CSD subtype requiring advanced repair; standardized classification is needed. In early CSP, hysteroscopy allows direct visualization and precise removal of chorionic tissue, minimizing hemorrhage and avoiding systemic therapy.
