N2  - Objective. To perform a comparative analysis of the short-and long-term outcomes of a new method of metroplasty in repeat cesarean section (CS) using an absorbable suture anchor. Patients and methods. This study included 202 women who were divided into two groups: group 1 (study group) included 102 patients who underwent repeat CS with metroplasty (a single-layer closure) using a self-locking suture anchor without reverse; group 2 (comparison group) included 100 patients who underwent repeat CS with metroplasty using traditional (polyglactin, multifilament) suture materials for closure of the uterine incision. The following parameters were analyzed: duration of surgery, volume of blood loss, clinical and laboratory test results, as well as ultrasound examination data in the postpartum period on days 3–4 after surgery and at 3 and 6 months. Results. The use of suture anchor during metroplasty in repeat CS reduced the duration of performing the uterine incision closure, the need for additional hemostatic sutures and the use of antifibrinolytic and hemostatic agents and did not increase the duration of surgery or total blood loss. Moreover, an ultrasound examination performed at 6 months after surgery showed better postoperative scar characteristics and no clinically significant niche (<3.0 mm) in the group where this technique was used. Conclusion. This study demonstrates clear advantages of using a new method of metroplasty in repeat CS using an absorbable suture anchor compared to the traditional method.
