N2  - Objectives: The rate of caesarean continues to increase around the world. Data from 2019/20 shows rates of 31% in England; in our own unit at James Cook University Hosiptal the rate is 34%. Long-term complications, including placenta accreta and scar ectopics, are associated with niche formation. Research from Genoa, Italy, shows that using V-Loc reduces the incidence of niche formation and adhesions. In November 2022 a single operator started to use V-Loc at elective caesarean at James Cook University Hospital. This study considers the outcomes of 150 cases using V-Loc. Methods: This paper compares data from the first year's use of V-Loc. Operative time to close, measured blood loss, and infection rates are examined. Initial data from the first 63 electives undertaken by the introducing operator were compared to data from the previous year. Results: Blood loss was reduced by 15% (512 ml, on average, to 434 ml). However, a confounding factor was noted, the use of TXA prophylaxis increased over the comparative time period. A further control group (57 cases) was identified of caesareans undertaken by consultants in 2023 (other than the single operator using V-Loc); this showed an average blood loss of 492 ml. Hospital admission/hospital outpatient management for infection/ PUO dropped from 9.6% to 1.5%. Looking at the second control group (57 cases as above) the hospital infection rate was 12.2%. In this time period, no cases of ileus were observed. Conclusions: This data indicated a reduction in blood loss and infection rates but no difference in operative time.
