N2  - Objective: To determine the prevalence of uterine abnormalities, particularly subtle endometrial lesions, in infertility patients who were not necessarily scheduled for assisted reproductive technologies (ART). Methods: A retrospective analysis was conducted on 2,200 consecutive infertility patients who underwent hysteroscopy at a single fertility centre. Patients were categorised as Group 1 (infertility only, n=1,875) and Group 2 (infertility with abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB), n=345). All procedures follow a standardised technique performed by experienced specialists. Hysteroscopic findings were systematically recorded using a predesigned form and classified as normal or abnormal, with abnormalities further subdivided into major and subtle lesions. Results: In Group 1, abnormal findings were detected in 961 patients (51%), in which 1,138 abnormalities were identified, including endometrial polyps (21%), submucous myomas (5%), malformations (9%), synechiae (11%), isthmocele (12%), retained product of conception (RPOC, 1%), thick hypervascularised endometrium (3%), cervical stenosis (9%), endocervical polyps (6%), and subtle lesions (23%). In Group 2, abnormal findings were detected in 280 patients (81%), in which 338 abnormalities were diagnosed, comprising endometrial polyps (33%), submucous myomas (9%), malformations (3%), synechiae (4%), isthmocele (7%), RPOC (2%), thick hypervascularised endometrium (5%), cervical stenosis (7%), endocervical polyps (9%), and subtle lesions (21%). Conclusion: Our study demonstrates a high prevalence of uterine abnormalities among infertile patients, particularly in those with concomitant AUB. This is largely attributable to the systematic documentation of subtle endometrial lesions. What is new? Our findings underscore the pivotal role of hysteroscopy in detecting subtle lesions, whose clinical significance in infertility, and specifically in ART, can only be elucidated following accurate diagnosis. Given the current absence of a standardised classification system for these lesions, we strongly advocate for the development of a comprehensive and universally accepted framework. Such initiative would minimise inter-observer variability and enhance the quality and consistency of evidence in future research.
