N2  - Study Objective: Ectopic pregnancy implantation in the cesarean scar niche is a rare form of non-tubal ectopic pregnancy. In advanced cases with concern for a placenta accreta spectrum diagnosis hysterectomy is commonly recommended, however this is an unappealing option for patients desiring fertility preservation. In this video, we demonstrate a robotic-assisted approach to laparoscopic removal of ectopic pregnancy in the cesarean scar niche, highlighting various techniques to assist with ureterolysis, bladder dissection, and decreasing intraoperative blood loss. Design: Stepwise demonstration using narrated video footage. Setting: Robot-equipped operating room with patient positioned in dorsal lithotomy. Patients or Participants: 33-year-old with a 12-week cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy with imaging concerning for placenta percreta desiring uterine preservation for retained fertility. Interventions: The patient underwent laparoscopic ectopic pregnancy removal through a robotic-assisted approach. Given concern for bladder involvement, indocyanine green was cystoscopically injected into the ureters at the opening of the case to aid ureterolysis and bladder dissection. The uterine arteries were temporarily ligated to minimize blood loss. Placental vessels perforating the bladder were carefully cauterized and dissected with monopolar scissors and bipolar energy. The ectopic pregnancy was removed, and the myometrial defect was closed in multiple layers to prevent cesarean scar niche recurrence. Measurements and Main Results: The patient underwent laparoscopic removal of ectopic pregnancy via robotic-assisted approach, with intraoperative course notable for 1500cc blood loss and transfusion of 3 units of packed red blood cells. The remainder of her postoperative course was unremarkable. This approach helped achieve her goal of uterine preservation, and at her six-week follow-up visit she was recovering well with no complaints. Conclusion: Though rare, cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy can be a life-altering diagnosis for patients. For advanced cases where uterine-sparing surgical management is planned, the above highlighted techniques can be employed to assist dissection and minimize intraoperative blood loss.
