N2  - Study Objective: to presents unique case of pregnancy in the rudimentary, non-communicating horn of a unicornuate uterus. Design: case report. Setting: patient was positioned in lithotomy position, general anesthesia. We used a zero angle, 10mm camera through the umbilicus and 3 more 5mm ports at the lower abdomen. We used harmonic scalpel for ligation and cutting, and a LINA morcellator to remove the specimen. We used vicryl -1-0 sutures for closing the uterine incision. Patients or Participants: 25 yrs old female. Interventions: laparoscopic surgery. Measurements and Main Results: following the patient after 3 months - she went through diagnostic hysteroscopy, and a few months later, she became spontaneously pregnant and proceeded successfully till the end of 3rd trimester to another CS. Conclusion: a successful treatment of unique ectopic pregnancy.
