N2  - OBJECTIVE: Asherman’s Syndrome (AS) is a rare disease (ORPHA: 137686) defined by the presence of intrauterine adhesions (IUAs) resulting in menstrual abnormalities, pelvic pain, infertility, and abnormal placentation. Autologous CD133+ Bone Marrow Derived Stem Cells (BMDSCs) were initially used to treat AS in a Phase I trial (Santamaria et al., HR 2016), alongside with the first comprehensive endometrial AS atlas at a single-cell resolution (Santamaria et al., Nat Commun 2023). As a result, autologous CD133+ BMDSCwas designated as Orphan Drug for the treatment of AS by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) and the FDA. Here, we report the outcome of a single-arm, phase I/II trial evaluating autologous CD133+ BMDSCs to treat moderate or severe AS (EudraCT Number: 2016-003975-23). MATERIALS AND METHODS: We enrolled a total of 20 infertile patients diagnosed of moderate (n=12) or severe AS (n=8). Despite previous operative hysteroscopies (mean 3.2; range 1-11), none reported significant improvement of their endometrial status nor had achieved ongoing pregnancy after AS diagnosis. All the patients underwent hormonal replacement therapy and a hysteroscopy to assess AS severity before and after treatment. Autologous CD133+ BMDSC were collected by apheresis after mobilization with filgastrim (10 mcg/kg/day 5 days) and instilled in both uterine arteries through left brachial artery catheterism. A mean of 122×106 CD133+cells (SD 48x106) were administered per patient. Subsequently, embryo transfer was performed completing a 15-month follow-up period. The primary objective was to assess safety, while the secondary objectives investigate effectiveness measuring endometrial thickness (2D/3D ultrasound), hysteroscopic grading (ASRM classification), and reproductive outcomes. Additionally, a total of 123,250 AS patient-derived cells (40,336 pre- vs 82,914 post-treatment) were assessed by single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNAseq) and pre- and post-treatment endometrial organoids were used to recreate an in vitro model of AS epithelium. RESULTS: No serious adverse events were reported during the 15-month follow-up period. Ultrasound examinations revealed improvements in endometrial thickness (3,8mm+/-0,58mm (pre) vs 5,29+/-0,77mm (post); p<0.05) as well as hysteroscopic grading (8+/-3,17(pre) vs 4,42+/-2,09 (post); p<0.05). Reproductive outcomes resulted in 30% of live birth rates (6/20), and 30% miscarriage rates (6/20) per treated patient. Implantation rates per embryo transferred were 31.7% (13/41). All newborns displayed normal development and Apgar scores with a mean weight of 3,510gr. At single cell level, this therapy partially reverts the dysfunctional pro-fibrotic, pro-inflammatory, and anti-angiogenic environment in the AS endometrium. Single-cell analysis of organoids also supported these transcriptomic features. CONCLUSIONS: Our results indicate that autologous CD133+ BMDSC therapy represents a safe and effective treatment for AS. IMPACT STATEMENT: This clinical results together with the characterization of their mechanisms of action at single cell level pave the way to a Phase III trial and eventual clinical translation of this advanced cell therapy. SUPPORT: This study was jointly supported by Human Uterus Cell Atlas Project from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation program under grant agreement No. 874867, PROMETEO/2018/161 from the Valencia Government, IDI-20201142 CDTI from the Spanish Government and th KY Cha Award for Stem Cell Technologies of the American Society of Reproductive Medicine (ASRM) REFERENCES: Santamaria, X. et al. Autologous cell therapy with CD133+ bone marrow-derived stem cells for refractory Asherman’s syndrome and endometrial atrophy: a pilot cohort study. Human Reproduction 31, 1087–1096 (2016). Santamaria X. et al. Decoding the endometrial niche of Asherman’s Syndrome at single-cell resolution. Nat Commun. 2023;14(1):5890.
