N2  - Introduction: Uterine NICHE, initially characterized by ISUOG in 2007, describes a defect in the lower uterine wall following cesarean section. Clinical manifestations include abnormal uterine bleeding, pelvic pain, and secondary infertility. Methods: We present a case of a woman with NICHE syndrome post two previous cesarean sections, reporting menstrual pain and secondary infertility. Sonography revealed a NICHE at the cesarean incision site. Laparoscopic NICHE repair, utilizing 3-D and fluorescence laparoscopy, was proposed and scheduled. Results: Upon overview, a hyperangiogenic area in the lower uterine segment and a cesarian scar, which appeared hyperplastic, were detected. No other intraabdominal pathologies were noted. We present following intraoperative steps for NICHE repair: 1. Step: An intrauterine manipulator was inserted. 2. Step: Peritoneum incision at the uterine-vesical space and caudal dissection of the bladder were performed without manipulating the uterine scar. 3. Step: Following intrauterine indocyanine-green (ICG) application, rapid spread of ICG within the abdomen confirmed the diagnosis of uterine subrupture. 4. Step: Complete excision of the NICHE region, revealing cervical cysts, was executed, followed by closure of the uterine incision with multiple double-layered sutures. 5. Step: Chromopertubation demonstrated patent tubes bilaterally, with no dye efflux from the uterine suture site. Three months postoperatively, sonographic evaluation showed no evidence of NICHE. Conclusion: Fluorescence laparoscopy aids in localizing uterine NICHE and tailoring surgical intervention. Women diagnosed with uterine NICHE should receive counseling regarding potential peripartum complications before planned pregnancy. Early detection and repair of uterine subrupture pre-pregnancy may mitigate maternal and fetal peripartum risks.
