N2  - Background: The incidence of caesarean scar pregnancy (CSP) is increasing reflecting the global increase in caesarean section rate. CSP is a part of abnormally invasive placenta spectrum disease, which leads to very high level of maternal morbidity and mortality. Case: A 43-year- old lady with a history of two previous caesarean sections and right-side salpingectomy for tubal ectopic pregnancy presented with crampy lower abdominal pain at 6 weeks pregnancy. According to the USS, there were two gestational sacs adjacent to caesarean section scar, one contained yolk sac and fetal pole with fetal heartbeat seen, another sac was empty. Myometrial thickness was 4 mm. At this point, it was explained that she is at high risk of abnormal invasive spectrum of placentation and miscarriage and plan was made for MRI and multi-disciplinary team (MDT) discussion. MRI demonstrated two opposing gestational sacs seen in lower uterine segment at CS scar niche with fetal pole in one, myometrial thickness was 3 mm and there were no signs of bladder invasion by gestational tissue. Patient opted out for termination of pregnancy via surgical evacuation under ultrasound guidance, which was performed at 9 weeks of pregnancy without difficulties with minimal blood loss. She had a follow-up scan in EPAU ten days after the procedure, which diagnosed 30 × 20 mm area of retained product of conception, beta-HCG was negative, and it was decided to manage it conservatively. The next USS (in 3 months) demonstrated no RPOC. Conclusion: It is the first case report of multiple caesarean scar pregnancy described in the literature.
