N2  - Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4) binds lipopolysaccharide (LPS) present on the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria and activates the innate immune response. A soluble form of TLR4 (sTLR4) has been recovered from human saliva and demonstrated to dampen the pro- duction of pro-inflammatory cytokines by macrophages. Prior work showed that sTLR4 was also present in amniotic fluid, endometrial glands, uterine tubes, and endocervical glands. However, there are no published studies exploring the presence or role of sTLR4 in lower gen- ital tract secretions; neither it is known whether sTLR4 levels fluctuate during the menstrual cycle. Reproductive-age women were enrolled in studies of bacterial vagi- nosis (BV) and vaginal microbiota. By using a chemiluminescent immunoassay, endocervical cytobrush and vaginal swab samples as well as primary genital epithelial cell supernatants were tested for the presence of sTLR4. Vaginal bacterial concentrations were assessed by species-specific quantitative PCR (qPCR). Immunoblotting was used to determine the molecular weight of sTLR4. A monocytic reporter cell line (THP1-Dual™) was used to evaluate a potential inhibitory effect of sTLR4-containing epithelial cell supernatants on LPS sig- naling. Gene-specific qPCR assays were employed to characterize sTLR4's anti-inflammatory effect on peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) challenged with LPS. We found sTLR4 in cervicovaginal secretions, with increased concen- trations in participants with endocervical ectopy [average: 6.992 ng/ml (n = 22)] compared to those without ectopy [average: 4.189 ng/ml (n = 40), p = 0.0152]. Participants sampled during the secretory phase had significantly lower concentrations of sTLR4 (average: 2.426 ng/ml, n = 18) than participants sampled during the proliferative phase (aver- age: 7.263 ng/ml, n = 16) or participants on hormonal contraceptives (average: 5.455 ng/ml, n = 19, p = 0.0034). Longitudinal analyses of sTLR4 concentrations in daily vaginal swabs (n = 6 donors) demon- strated that sTLR4 reached the highest levels near ovulation. sTLR4 levels were not correlated with the presence of BV, age, concentra- tion of vaginal Gram-negative bacteria, or genetic variation in TLR4. Supernatants from primary endocervical cell lines had higher levels of sTLR4 than those derived from ectocervical or vaginal cells. We demonstrated that sTLR4 is a ~100 kDa polypeptide, corresponding to the entire TLR4 ectodomain. In THP1-Dual™cells, the interferon- regulatory factor pathway was inhibited when LPS was preincubated with endocervical cell supernatants (% inhibition: 73.8 +/- 12.4, n = 5 experiments). TNF-α mRNA induction in PBMCs was dampened when LPS was premixed with endocervical cell supernatants (n = 7 donors, p = 0.0285). Work with siRNA is ongoing to prove that the inhibitory component in endocervical cell supernatants is fully or in part due to sTLR4. Collectively, we found sTLR4 in cervicovaginal secretions, with increased concentration of sTLR4 during the proliferative phase of the menstrual cycle, and in participants with endocervical ectopy. The levels of sTLR4 were not influenced by age, diagnosis of BV, vaginal microbiota, or genetic variation in TLR4. Columnar endocervical cells and, to a lower extent ectocervical and vaginal cells, produce sTLR4 in vitro. Exposure to LPS demonstrated that endocervical cell sTLR4- containing supernatants elicited anti-inflammation. These results point to an unappreciated form of innate immune regulation in the cervi- covaginal niche, potentially opening new avenues for understanding inflammatory diseases in the reproductive tract.
