N2  - Introduction: As the rate of Cesarean sections continue to elevate, Cesarean section scar pregnancies are becoming more common. This unique ectopic pregnancy can be missed if early diagnosis is not completed, due to persist longer pregnancy that can lead to catastrophic consequences. Early diagnosis allows effective treatment with good outcomes. Treatment is recommended based on the desire for future fertility and the gestational age at diagnosis and include surgical and conservative treatments. Surgical approaches include hysterectomy when future fertility is not desired, and hysteroscopy or excision of the pregnancy with uterine niche repair, depending on the residual myometrial thickness at the area of the uterine scar. Conservative treatments include: Medical (Methotrexate- MTX), uterine artery embolization (UAE) and balloon catheter. Surgical interventions have been recently associated with shorter BHCG surveillance, shorter hospitalization and faster recovery; however, data are still limited. Objective: To compare the characteristics and outcomes of surgical and conservative treatments for Cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy. Methods: This is a retrospective study including all women diagnosed with Cesarean scar pregnancy in a single tertiary medical center, between 2019 to 2023. Women treated surgically (including: hysterectomy, hysteroscopy and excision of the pregnancy with uterine niche repair) were compared to women treated conservatively (including: MTX, IUE, balloon catheter). Demographics, medical history, obstetrical and gynecological characteristics, presenting symptoms, imaging findings, management, operation characteristics and follow up were collected from electronic medical files. Results: 42 women were diagnosed with Cesarean scar pregnancy during the study period, of them 33(79%) were treated surgically and 9(21%) were treated conservatively. Median age was 33(30-36) year old and median BMI was 31(27-35) kg/m2 with no statistical differences in-between the groups. Most of the women had previous 2 Cesarean sections (n=12, 28%). 63% of the women had no presenting symptom while diagnosed. On transvaginal ultrasound examination, embryo was demonstrated in 65.9% and cardiac activity was observed in 51%. The median gestational age at diagnosis and BHCG levels were comparable between the surgical and conservative groups (p=0.22, p=0.61 respectively). Among all treatments, the most common was excision of the pregnancy with uterine niche repair that was performed in 14/42 (33%) of the women followed by hysterectomy (n=13/42, 31%). Median hospitalization length was 2(1-3) and 3(1-4) days, for the surgical and conservative treatments, respectively(p=0.51). Hemoglobin level before discharge was similar between the groups (p=0.09). Only one woman in each group visited emergency room before follow up visit (p=0.31). BHCG levels on post operation follow up was significantly lower in the surgical compared to the conservative group (p=0.06). Conclusions: Cesarean scar pregnancy might be asymptomatic and should be suspected in order to allow early diagnosis. Surgical and conservative treatments are feasible options, with similar hospitalization length; however, significantly faster decrease in BHCG levels is expected for the surgical intervention group. Disclosure: No.
