N2  - Introduction: Cesarean section is one of the most performed surgery around the world. Not only its rate is on the rise but also associated complications such as isthmocele and the related risk factors for developing isthmocele are unclear. Objective: To analyze factors associated with the onset of isthmocele regardless of the presence of clinical symptoms. Methods: Methods: Cross-sectional study with 90 women with a history of cesarean delivery assessed from 2020 to 2022. Isthmocele was a composite variable diagnosed by transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and/or diagnostic hysteroscopy (DxHys) and were asked about clinical symptoms, sociodemographic and obstetrical history and quality of life by the WHO-QOL questionnaire. Details from the isthmocele by MRI and US were analyzed. Univariate and multivariate analysis (odds ratio (OR) plus 95% confidence intervals - CI) were performed to seek factors associated with the presence of isthmocele (5% significance level). Results: Results: The prevalence of isthmocele after combining MRI, TVUS and DxHys was 63.3% (n=57). Women with isthmocele presented a higher body mass index (BMI) measured during delivery (32.70±6.07 vs. 28.28±9.86 kg/m2;p<0.05) than women without isthmocele. Other sociodemographic variables, obstetrical history and WHO-QOL subdomains did not differ between groups. Within women with isthmocele, the residual myometrial mantle had an average of 4.97±1.57cm. Uterine volume was higher in the isthmocele group (103.95 vs. 81.34 cm3; p=0.08), but with no statistical difference. Multivariate analysis (logistic regression) has reported that the factors associated with isthmocele were: higher BMI during delivery (aOR=1.26[1.07-1.49];p<0.05); longer interdelivery interval (aOR=1.22[1.03-1.46];p=0.02) number of cesareans (aOR=2.16[1.16-4.01];p=0.02) and other surgeries (aOR=0.10[0.02-0.57]. Conclusions: Conclusion: A high prevalence of isthmocele was found. Women with previous cesarean section, with higher BMI during delivery and longer interdelivery interval were risk factors for the presence of isthmocele. Disclosure: No.
