N2  - Introduction: The number of cases of placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) disorder has been increasing with the increase in older pregnancies, cesarean section, and in-vitro fertilization. In addition, PAS without placenta previa is difficult to diagnose prior to delivery , and opportunities for multidisciplinary treatment may be missed. In addition, uterine artery embolization and total hysterectomy are associated with less or loss of fertility. The TURIP (Tourniquet, Uterine Inversion and Placental dissection) method was developed as a surgical method to solve these problems. Methods: The TURIP method has three key points: first, the uterine isthmus is ligated with a 6 Fr Neraton catheter to control bleeding; second, intravenous nitroglycerin 100 μg is administered to relax and invert the uterus, and the adhered placenta is removed under direct vision; and third, two or three layers of sutures are used to stop bleeding and simultaneously conceal the endometrium, which is located at the adhesion site, to ensure that only the normal endometrium remains. After that, removing the tourniquet and confirming hemostasis, 100 μg of nitroglycerin is administered again to recover uterine inversion. A uterine hemostatic balloon is inserted into the uterus and filled with 150 mL of distilled water to ensure endometrial hemostasis. TURIP procedure was performed in 10 cases of PAS without placenta previa not diagnosed preoperatively from September 2019 to June 2023 at our institution (7 performed during cesarean section, 2 after vaginal delivery, and 1 after manual detachment of the placenta during a cesarean section.) Results: Median age, week of delivery, operative time, and intraoperative blood loss were 40 (34-44) years, 38 (29-39) weeks, 113 (80-228) minutes, and 2,568 (1,850-8,213) ml, respectively. In all patients, there were no perioperative complications and successful uterine preservation was achieved at one period. The median follow-up period was 14 (4-49) months. All patients underwent magnetic resonance imaging at 3 months postoperatively, and the endometrium was smooth with no adhesion and niche, and with no thinning of the muscular layer. one of the three cases that wanted to conceive, became pregnant and delivered by cesarean section without recurrence of PAS. In all cases, menstruation resumed and there were no dysmenorrhea and postmenstrual spotting. Conclusion: The TURIP procedure is suitable for fertility preservation of undiagnosed PAS without placenta previa because it is simple and does not require advanced techniques or not require extensive training, instruments, or equipment. It is applicable not only to cases diagnosed at the time of cesarean section, but also to emergency hemostasis after vaginal delivery and rebleeding after cesarean section.
