N2  - Introduction and objectives: The combined therapy of cesarean scar pregnancy (CSP), implanted within a dehiscent scar (“niche”), with the use of intra-amniotic and/or intravenous methotrexate (MTX), followed by hysteroscopic removal of products of conception, is effectively conducted at our center. Nevertheless, cases of intraoperative hemorrhage are difficult to predict. Since the prevention of excessive bleeding is critical to optimizing patient care, a study was planned to assess the factors affecting the severity of intraoperative bleeding. Methods: A retrospective tertiary-center observational study included women undergoing combined CSP treatment, preceded by the consent of the Bioethics Committee for termination of pregnancy. Spearman's correlation analysis and the Mann-Whitney U test of differences were performed to check whether blood loss was related to women's characteristics. Results: The study involved 40 women aged 29-41 years, with a gestational age of 6-12 weeks. Significant blood loss, defined as a decrease in hemoglobin (Hb) >2g/dl on the postoperative day 1, was found in 15/40 (37.5%), estimated blood loss >500 ml in 8/40 (20%), and the need for packed red blood cells transfusion in 9/40 (22.5%) women. There was no correlation between gestational age (rS=-0.03, p=0.64), number of cesarean sections (rS=-0.14, p=0.37), BMI (rS <0.01, p=0.99), day of surgery after MTX administration (rS= -0.12, p=0.46), positive fetal heartbeat before MTX administration (p=0.11), dynamics of B-human chorionic gonadotropin concentrations (increasing/decreasing) before MTX administration (p=0.81), and the decrease in Hb concentration on the postoperative day 1. Similarly, there was no significant correlation between the above variables and the volume of estimated intraoperative blood loss or the need for packed red blood cells transfusion. Conclusions: The results do not indicate significant predisposing factors for increased bleeding. A study with a larger sample could provide new insights.
