N2  - Objectives: This study aims to compare the diagnostic accuracy of hysterosalpingography (HSG) and outpatient hysteroscopy in patients with suspected tubal obstruction. Materials and methods: This prospective cohort study was conducted at an infertility clinic in a tertiary university hospital between 2020 and 2023. Patients who underwent HSG as part of their infertility work-up and were diagnosed with either unilateral or bilateral tubal obstruction were included in the study. Office hysteroscopy was performed on these patients, with the surgeon blinded to the HSG diagnosis. During hysteroscopy, air bubbles were injected into the uterine cavity under ultrasound guidance to assess tubal patency. The presence of visible air bubbles in each adnexa and visible fluid collection in the Douglas pouch were considered as signs of tubal potency. Visual analog pain scores were recorded for both procedures, and the diagnostic outcomes were compared. Results: A total of 60 patients diagnosed with tubal obstruction were included in the study. Office hysteroscopy was performed following the diagnosis. The tubal diagnosis based on HSG was bilateral obstruction (60%), unilateral obstruction (23%), and bilateral patency (17%). Uterine cavity results showed normal findings in 83% of cases, arcuate uterus in 8%, uterine polyp in 3%, bicorporeal uterus in 2%, and Asherman syndrome in the remaining cases. The tubal diagnosis based on hysteroscopy was bilateral obstruction (35%), unilateral obstruction (22%), and bilateral patency (43%). Uterine cavity results showed normal findings in 75% of cases, arcuate uterus in 5%, septate uterus in 3%, uterine polyp in 11%, isthmocele in 2%, and endometritis in 3%. There were statistically significant differences in both tubal and uterine cavity diagnoses between the two procedures. Hysteroscopy identified bilateral tubal patency in 32% of patients initially diagnosed with tubal obstruction, and 10% of patients with reported normal uterine cavity on HSG were diagnosed with uterine pathology during hysteroscopy. Conclusion: Assessing tubal patency is a crucial aspect of infertility work-up. While HSG is commonly used as the initial procedure, alternatives such as saline-hystero-sonography and hysteroscopy exist. It is known that false-positive results can occur during HSG due to bilateral proximal tubal spasm. In such cases, office hysteroscopy may be considered as an option to reassess tubal patency, offering higher diagnostic accuracy for both uterine cavity and tubal conditions, as well as lower pain scores. It could potentially be considered as a first-line option in selected cases.
