N2  - The last decades have seen a huge increase in the number of Cesarean section deliveries (CS) worldwide and it seems that this trend will not slow down (Sandall et al., 2018; Betran et al., 2021). Evidence mounts that CS has a negative impact on future fertility and the long-term sequelae are now a focus of research. An isthmocele represents an inadequate healing of the myometrium at the site of the CS incision and is defined through its ultrasonographic finding as a triangular, anechogenic area at the site of incision (Bij de Vaate et al., 2011). In clinical routine, isthmoceles appear in different shapes and sizes. Depending on the type of screening, the reported prevalence of isthmoceles is 7 – 69%. Most commonly, isthmoceles are diagnosed by vaginal ultrasound, saline hysterosonography or by MRI. Though an isthmocele can be asymptomatic, the most reported symptom is post-menstrual spotting. Other common symptoms are dysmenorrhoe, dyspareunie and secondary infertility. Symptoms are caused partly by an accumulation of menstrual blood in the niche and a disturbed contractility in the scar region. Secondary infertility can result from impairment of the sperm ascension through the disturbed contractility and the presence of blood / mucus in the isthmocele. Women with an isthmocele, undergoing ovarian stimulation for IVF/ICSI, have a risk of developing intracavitary fluid during ovarian stimulation (Lawrenz et al., 2020), the risk being correlated with the size of the isthmocele and the number of previous CS. It is well described that intracavitary fluid (ICF) reduces the chance of a pregnancy after embryo transfer. However, if an euploid frozen embryo transfer is performed and ICF is excluded, the negative effect of the previous CS on the outcome can be overcome (Bayram et al., 2022).
