N2  - Background: ASRM provides clinical practice guidelines for embryo transfer and there are many well designed trials that assess what variables may be helpful in optimizing outcomes for patients. However, there are additional variables that deserve further attention in order to predict best practice for clinicians and optimal outcomes for patients. Objective: The objective of this study was to analyze factors that may affect success of embryo transfer. Materials and Methods: A retrospective review of all transfer cycles between April 2022 and August 2022 was performed using logistic and multiple regression analyses, based on the type of variable. A total of 1573 patients were included, with 23 transfer variables analyzed. Transfer were excluded only if no data was collected on that date of transfer. Data was retrieved from the electronic medical record system, with input of variables into the system by the ultrasonographer and nurse present during the transfer. Primary outcome was ongoing pregnancy defined as the presence of a sustained implantation with fetal heart beat at the time of discharge to outside obstetrical care around eight weeks of gestational age. Secondary outcomes were chemical pregnancy and clinical pregnancy as defined by ultrasound evidence of intrauterine pregnancy. Result(s): Of 1573 patients, there were 1210 chemical pregnancies (76.9%), 1059 clinical pregnancies (67.3%) and 913 discharged with ongoing live pregnancies to obstetrical care (58.0%). The mean oocyte age was 34.1 (range 20.7-45.6). The mean maternal age at embryo transfer was 36.1 (range 22.1-52.9) Trophectoderm biopsy with PGT was performed on 90% of all embryos transferred. Table 1 delineates the categorical variables that were found to have a statistically significant association with the transfer outcomes. Continuous variables that statistically predicted all transfer outcomes included: depth of catheter during transfer (p= <0.01 chemical, 0.01 clinical, 0.01 ongoing), if and number of times the embryo was returned to the embryologist prior to transfer (p=<0.01, 0.02, 0.04), and total time of embryo transfer (p= <0.01, <0.01, 0.02). Variables that did not reach significance included flash presence, flash location, flash speed, catheter type, tip location, quality of image, cesarean section niche, endometrial preparation, first cycle, mucus on tip, time between thaw and transfer, and time between the physician sitting down and requesting to load the embryo. [Formula presented] Conclusion(s): This study is the first to look at additional variables that can impact transfer technique and optimize outcomes for patients. Surprisingly, the presence of a flash did not seem to affect success rates of embryo transfer. However, modifiable techniques such as the amount of time from the physician’s hand to the transfer and the depth of transfer, were significant. Financial Support: None
