N2  - Introduction: Cesarean scar pregnancy (CSP)refers to a rare form of an ectopic pregnancy that is implanted on or in a scar of a prior cesarean birth. It occurs in approximately 1:2000 pregnancies. If not detected on an early stage, a CSP can potentially cause life-threatening fetal and maternal complications such as uterine rupture or severe hemorrhage. Although there are no established guidelines, the existing literature suggests a termination of the pregnancy with either a conservative or an operative treatment or a combination of both, depending on the gestational week, the hemodynamic stability and the desire for future fertility. Here we present the case of a conservatively treated cesarean scar pregnancy. Case Report: A 31-year-old Caucasian G2 P1 with medical history of a cesarean section 3 years ago presented to our clinic at the 5th gestational week with vaginal bleeding.The transvaginal ultrasound showed a gestational sack with embryo of 1.8mm without heartbeat localized directly above the internal cervical os. Due to the presence of hematoma on the uterine fundus we primarily interpreted the finding as dislocated abortive tissue as part of an incipient abort.A sonographic follow-up after 5 days revealed a CSP with progressive gestational sack and a vital embryo of 4mm located on the uterine scar.Due to a myometrium thickness of 11mm and good clinical status we proposed an expectative management. The patient came 2 days later with contractions and vaginal bleeding. The pregnancy was vital. Myometrial thickness underneath the gestational sack was decreased to a minimum of 1.5mm, forming a niche on the lower uterine segment. The fundal hematoma increased in size, measuring 30×30mm. The patient was hemodynamically stable. Given the high morbidity risk we indicated a termination of the pregnancy. It was managed conservatively with administration of 600μg mifepristone orally followed by 800mg prostaglandin vaginally after 2 days. The follow-up sonography after 1 week showed remaining residual tissue of 16×19mm with slight perfusion, that persisted for 6 weeks. Additionally we performed regular controls of beta-hCG level until it was below the reference range. After 11 months no residual tissue was detected. Conclusion: CSP is a rare medical condition with especially high maternal (and fetal) complication rates. Despite the lack in established guidelines, experts advise against a prolongation of the pregnancy. The further treatment depends on the hemodynamic stability, gestational age and desire for future fertility.
