N2  - Introduction: Menstruation is characterised by high concentrations of inflammatory mediators and immune cells which are known to play a pivotal role in tissue breakdown. Endometrial repair following menstruation is essential for women's reproductive health and macrophages are believed to be key mediators of this process. Maintenance of the macrophage niche is required for healthy tissue function but we lack fundamental knowledge about how this is regulated in the endometrium. Given that macrophage dysfunction is associated with reproductive health disorders, we sought to characterise the contribution of macrophages and their precursors (monocytes) to endometrial repair in mice undergoing induced menstruation. Methods: We used a mouse model of induced menstruation to investigate the role of monocytes/macrophages in endometrial tissue breakdown and repair. Immune cells were isolated from uterine tissues from mice undergoing active repair or following repair/resolution. Immune cell composition and dynamics were assessed using multi-parameter fow cytometry and established subset markers. Monocytes were depleted using genetic (Ccr2-/-) or antibody-mediated (anti-CCR2 mAb; MC-21) approaches and repair responses assessed by fow cytometry and immunohistochemistry. Results: Flow cytometry revealed that monocytes increased during active repair and were more abundant than tissue resident macrophages during active repair (p<0.0001). Following resolution of repair, tissue resident macrophages increased and were more abundant than monocytes (p<0.001). Monocyte depletion prior to repair signifcantly reduced Ly6C+Ccr2+ monocytes in the blood (p<0.0001) and subsequently decreased monocytes (Ly6C+Ccr2+, p<0.05) and tissue resident macrophages (CD64+F480+, p<0.01) in uterine tissues. Monocyte depletion was associated with disrupted endometrial repair characterised by increased bleeding at menses, altered immune cell composition in the uterus, and reduced re-epithelialisation. Conclusion: Monocytes are abundant during experimentally-induced menstruation and systemic depletion disrupts endometrial repair processes. These novel data ofer new insight into the important role of monocytes and macrophages in menstruation and women's reproductive health.
