N2  - Category: Gynaecological radiology Purpose: Caesarean section (CS) delivery is associated with reduced subsequent fertility compared with vaginal delivery (VD) and there is a higher incidence of CS in women who have undergone assisted reproductive treatment (ART). This study aims to determine if women referred for hysterosalpingography (HSG) with a history of secondary subfertility and CS demonstrates agreement with these published statements and to identify potential causative factors that may contribute to the subfertility. Methods and materials: Data relating to gynaecological, obstetric and medical history and HSG outcomes was collected retrospectively from the imaging requests, reports and case notes of women referred for HSG (Research Ethics Committee approved). Information from 605 HSG referrals was collated and analysed with respect to age, parity, previous ART, mode of delivery and pathology demonstrated. Results: 605 women, mean age 36 years, 254 primary, 351 secondary subfertility, were included. Of 616 pregnancies, 100 (38%) of the 263 live births were delivered by CS. Previous ART was associated with 48% of VD and 61% of CS. The HSG was abnormal in 54% VD and 72% CS cases. Abnormalities included CS niche, unilateral or bilateral tubal occlusion, fibroids, polyps and Asherman’s syndrome. Conclusion: This analysis agrees with other published data. HSG provides a simple and effective method of identifying the presence or absence of a CS niche, which can present with abnormal uterine bleeding, dysmenorrhoea, dyspareunia, caesarean scar ectopic pregnancies, or secondary subfertility and other treatable uterine and tubal abnormalities that may contribute to reduced fertility.
