N2  - Objective: To illustrate the steps of a robotic-assisted laparoscopic excision of a large cesarean scar pregnancy. Description: A cesarean scar pregnancy is defined as placental implantation within the scar of a previous cesarean delivery. True incidence is unknown though it is estimated as about 6% of all ectopic pregnancies in women with prior cesarian section. Ultrasound is the primary image modality used for the diagnosis. Most cases are asymptomatic at the time of diagnosis, but if left untreated can result in uterine dehiscence, placenta accreta spectrum, or life-threatening hemorrhage. Surgical excision with a wedge resection is usually recommended for patients with thin overlying myometrium. In this video, we present a case of a 41-year-old G6P3023 with a history of three prior cesarian sections and a known isthmocele who was diagnosed with a cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy. She was counseled for a complete surgical resection given the extremely thin overlying serosa and proximity to the bladder. Key surgical steps are illustrated including: - Development of the bladder flap - Identification of the borders of the pregnancy - Injection of vasopressin - Use of a uterine manipulator with cervical cup for traction - Dissection to the base of the pregnancy in the uterine cavity - Circumferential excision of an intact pregnancy - Full thickness myometrial closure in multiple layers - Use of an adhesion barrier method Conclusion: Robotic-assisted laparoscopic excision is a feasible and effective treatment for cesarean scar pregnancy.
