N2  - Objective: The objectives are to discuss the etiology, common symptoms and management options of a c-section scar niche as well as to demonstrate a hysteroscopic technique using a shaver to excise the niche. Description: The presented case is a 35-year-old female with history of 3 low transverse c-sections undergoing in vitro fertilization (IVF) for secondary infertility. During initial IVF monitoring, ultrasound revealed a normal uterus and no c-section scar niche was identified. However, during subsequent IVF stimulation after a failed transfer, fluid was noted in the myometrium and a c-section scar niche was diagnosed. The niche was seen on multiple ultrasounds and the patient was advised to undergo resection before embryo transfer. A hysteroscopic approach was recommended given the thickness of the overlying myometrium. During hysteroscopy, the endometrial cavity, cesarean section scar niche and the internal cervical os need to be visualized in order to undergo safe niche excision. This procedure is performed using the TruClear dense tissue shaver mini device and is done under ultrasound guidance to avoid perforating the uterus. The myometrium proximal and distal to the c-section scar niche need to be shaved in order to allow better communication of the niche with the endometrial cavity and avoid fluid accumulation that may be toxic to the embryo. After completely resecting the niche, the patient is advised to wait 3 months to allow the myometrium to heal before attempting embryo transfer. Additionally, ultrasound postoperative is advised to assess for c-section niche resolution. Conclusion: In conclusion, a hysteroscopic approach to a c-section scar niche can be safely performed using an advanced tissue shaver technique.
