N2  - OBJECTIVE: To determine the efficacy of niche repair to improve fertility in patients with niche. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Retrospective cohort study conducted at a university-affiliated private practice. Inclusion criteria were women of reproductive age with secondary infertility and diagnosis of niche desiring treatment. They underwent niche repair performed by a single high skilled surgeon. Data was collected from follow up and medical records to assess symptoms, attempts to conceive and pregnancy. RESULTS: 80 women underwent niche repair for infertility. Median age 36 years with1-2 previous cesarean delivery. Most had 5 years of secondary infertility. Post-surgery 50 patients still desired fertility. 70% achieved pregnancy post niche repair. No patient reported long-term post-surgical complication. Of the pregnant patients, 20% delivered full term, 8% miscarried and 72% have ongoing pregnancy. CONCLUSIONS: Laparoscopic repair (with or without robotic assistance) of niche should be considered in patients experiencing secondary infertility after a Cesarean Section to improve fertility IMPACT STATEMENT: Caesareans can result in an indentation of the myometrium at the site of the Caesarean scar, called a niche. Symptoms of niche include abnormal uterine bleeding, dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic pain, dyspareunia and secondary infertility. Fibrotic tissue in the niche acts like a valve, leading to accumulation of blood in this reservoir-like area. Accumulated blood can ultimately degrade cervical mucus and sperm quality, as well as inhibit sperm transport leading to infertility. If women with a niche conceive, they are at higher risk of cesarean scar ectopic and potentially uterine rupture. Unfortunately, niches are under-diagnosed and hence undertreated. They can be repaired via laparoscopic or hysteroscopic approach.
