N2  - OBJECTIVE: To assess the relationship between cesarean scar niche and postmenstrual spotting through MRI evaluation. MATERIALS AND METHODS: A cohort study was performed between October 2019 and November 2020 included women diagnosed with cesarean scar niche by transvaginal sonography 6-12 months after the cesarean section (CS). They were divided into two groups; group (A) presented with postmenstrual spotting and group (B) had no spotting. MRI was done for evaluation of the shape, length, width, depth, volume of the niche and the residual scar thickness were measured. The relative change percentage in myometrial thickness in relation to the posterior wall was assessed. RESULTS: The study included 62 women (31 in each group). Sixteen women (51.6%) in group (B) had droplet shape niche, while 18 women (58.1%) in group (A) have semi-circular niche (p=0.02). Group (B) has significantly lower niche length (5.02±2.10 vs. 9.38±3.06 mm; p< 0.001), depth (3.23±0.99 vs. 6.95±3.16 mm; p<0.001), width (9.87±1.84 vs.15.78±3.94 mm; p<0.001), and volume (0.07±0.05 vs. 0.57±0.47 cm3; p<0.001). Group (A) had significantly lower minimal residual scar thickness (2.58±1.30 vs. 6.26±3.97 mm; p<0.001). Relative change percentage was significantly higher in group (A) (79.84±9.24 vs. 57.26±10.84%; p< 0.001). The duration of post-menstrual spotting has weak positive significant correlation with niche length (r= 0.41, p<0.001), width (r=0.51, p<0.001), and volume (r=0.49, p<0.001). CONCLUSIONS: MRI is a reliable method for assessment of niche. The niche length, depth, width, volume, the minimal residual scar thickness and the relative change percentage in myometrial thickness are significantly related to the occurrence of postmenstrual spotting. IMPACT STATEMENT: CS niche could be evaluated precisely in women presented with postmenstrual spotting through MRI.
