N2  - Background: Caesarean scar pregnancies (CSP) have become an increasingly common phenomenon in recent decades, following an increase in caesarean section rates. CSP carry a risk of abnormal placentation disorders, uterine rupture and haemorrhage which may result in the need for hysterectomy or even death. There is a need for consensus on the management of these pregnancies and strategies to optimize long-term outcomes for affected patients. Case: A 34-year-old female with two prior caesarean sections presented to our tertiary centre with a history of abdominal pain and heavy per vaginal bleeding. A pelvic ultrasound at our facility showed a live caesarean scar pregnancy. The patient decided to terminate the pregnancy and, due to a desire for future pregnancy, initially opted for medical therapy with KCl injection into the gestational sac and long course methotrexate. Medical therapy was unsuccessful and so was treated surgically with laparoscopy, dilatation and curettage with resection of caesarean scar niche. Discussion: In some cases of CSP, the pregnancy may implant upon a dehiscent scar, called a 'niche'. Uterine niches may be associated with post-menstrual spotting, dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic pain, subfertility, CSP and malplacentation disorders. Laparoscopic resection of the uterine niche is performed in symptomatic non-pregnant women and this approach has shown to improve symptoms and overall quality of life. Laparoscopic resection of the uterine niche can be performed opportunistically as part of surgical management of CSP to prevent long-term complications and improve fertility.
