N2  - Caesarean scar pregnancy (CSP) results from implantation in the caesarean section scar niche and is thought to sit on the spectrum of placenta accrete spectrum disorders. The incidence of this condition is increasing with increasing rates of primary caesarean delivery and represents six per cent of pregnancies in women with a previous caesarean section. Implantation in the caesarean scar defect is associated with signifcant risk of maternal morbidity including haemorrhage and hysterectomy. Due to the increasing uptake and availability of quality frst trimester ultrasound imaging and sensitive beta human chorionic gonadotropin measurement, the diagnosis of CSP is increasingly made in the frst trimester. The optimal management strategy is yet to be determined but earlier diagnosis allows for a considered and individualised approach to management which includes expectant, medical and surgical options.
