N2  - Over the past decade there has been significant advancement in understanding the multitude of ways naltrexone interacts with human physiology. The two main theories purported in literature are suppression of cytokine production by glial cells and transient blockade of the mu- opioid receptor. This case report highlights a chronic pain patient that sustained a wean from high dose opioid regimen utilizing low dose naltrexone. Her daily pain score, as calculated using the Defense and Veterans Pain Rating Scale, was lower after she was weaned from opioids. Introduction: Over the past decade there has been significant advancement in understanding the mechanisms in which naltrexone interacts with human physiology. Over the same time course, the incidence and prevalence of fibromyalgia and other associated chronic pain conditions such as chronic pelvic pain have been steadily increasing. According to the Center for Disease Control, as of 2018 over 4 million adults in the US 1suffer from a chronic pain condition; this equates to over 2% of the adult population1. The current treatments for chronic pain revolve around ameliorating symptoms with modalities like cognitive behavior therapy, physical therapy, nutrition, and medications such as gabapentin and lidocaine infusions. Since the mainstay of treatment does not target the underlying pathophysiological condition, there has been constant research into more effective methods to manage these conditions. Low Dose Naltrexone (LDN) is a medication that has been gaining popularity in the treatment of chronic pain over the past decade2. Naltrexone, a cousin of naloxone, is most simply thought of as a longer acting version of naloxone and is approved by the FDA for the same indications. Naltrexone is commonly prescribed in a 50mg dose for opiate overdose and recovery from addiction to alcohol. Naltrexone at very small doses, 3 mg to 4.5 mg, produces significant benefits in chronic pain. The mechanism of action is debated in the literature. Two of the proposed pathways are presented below. The first proposed mechanism is that naltrexone attenuates the production of proinflammatory cytokines via suppressive effects on microglia cells. Unlike other glial cells, microglia are the resident immune cells in the central nervous system. Microglia are activated by inflammatory triggers. Once activated, microglia continue the proinflammatory cascade by producing both proinflammatory and excitatory factors which attract other immune cells and lead to neuroinflammation. Neuroinflammation leads to hypersensitivity, maintenance, and prolongation of neuropathic and nociceptive pain inputs5. Since conditions such as fibromyalgia may involve chronic glial cell activation and subsequent production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, it is believed that suppressing glial cell activation will dampen the chronic pain4. Another suggested mechanism of action of naltrexone is through transient blockade of the mu-opioid receptor. The body responds to transiently blocking mu-receptors by upregulating endogenous opioid production and mu- receptors6. The opioid receptor upregulation effect has been demonstrated multiple times in the literature7,8. Thus, the use of LDN may enhance analgesia by allowing the body to temporarily block the receptors responsible for pain transmission and upregulate intrinsic pain reduction mechanisms. We present a unique case of opiate elimination with adequate pain control in an opiate tolerant individual over a short time course. This result was attained using a personally tailored comprehensive approach and several non-opiate modalities, the primary of which was LDN. The patient presented was opiate free for at least the next 6 months, until she transferred her care. Case Report: A 45 year-old obese caucasian female, presented to Naval Medical Center Portsmouth (NMCP) for exacerbation of her chronic pain. Her past medical history was significant for chronic pelvic pain (CPP), fibromyalgia (FM) and interstitial cystitis (IC). The genesis of her pain was an unrecognized uterine perforation, complicated by intraperitoneal silver nitrate infiltration of her peritoneum. She was well known to the pain clinic, and was admitted to NMCP frequently for exacerbation of her pain. Her medication regimen was extensive; she was maintained on high-dose OxyContin with breakthrough oxycodone, which she regularly took daily. The total prescribed morphine equivalent was 242mg/day, but this is believed to underrepresent her total daily usage due to frequent requests for breakthrough pain medication refills. At the time of admission, she had developed opiate tolerance, iatrogenic gastroparesis, and opioid induced hyperalgesia. She was evaluated and admitted to the internal medicine team for pain control and was started on intravenous dilaudid. Her pain was not well controlled, and the chronic pain service was consulted. On day one of admission the pain service initiated a ketamine infusion protocol to more effectively control her pain, reduce her opiate requirement and reduce hyperalgesia. The ketamine protocol was started at 2.0 mcg/kg/min and her opiate requirement began to decrease. By hospital day 2, the patient was weaned to <90% of her daily opiate requirement, and a multidisciplinary team discussion with the patient and her husband occurred. Using shared decision making, it was decided to initiate a trial of Low Dose Naltrexone (LDN). She was informed of the requirements for LDN therapy, which consisted of a 5-day hospital stay and elimination of all opiates. Her consent was obtained and therapy started. The patient was started on 2.5mg naltrexone on the evening of hospital day 3. Her ketamine infusion at that time was 4.5mcg/kg/ml. A baseline 7 mm/hr erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), a nonspecific marker for inflammation, was obtained prior to initiation of treatment. It has been reported in literature that a patient with a reduction in ESR after initiation of LDN shows a greater response to the medication2. Over the ensuing days, she noted a significant reduction in pain on the Defense and Veteran's Pain Rating Scale (DVPRS) as seen in Figure 1, that was sustained after discontinuation of the ketamine infusion. On the day of discharge her ESR was measured again and had reduced to 3mm/hr. After discharge, she continued LDN and was able to maintain her wean from her oral opioid medications. Her average pain level prior to her admission ranged from six to nine out of ten. Since her last admission, her average pain level was less than or equal to five, as displayed in Figure 2. [Figure presented] [Figure presented] Discussion: Over the past decade the incidence and prevalence of chronic pain patients with fibromyalgia have been steadily increasing in the United States1. The data suggests that the current treatment modalities for chronic pain provide only temporizing measures and do not address the underlying pathophysiology. As previously stated, given the wide variety of inflammatory factors produced by activated microglia (e.g., proinflammatory cytokines, substance P, nitric oxide, and excitatory amino acids), a range of symptoms that include chronic pain share the same pathophysiological mechanism of central inflammation3,9. Additionally, the “opioid rebound” caused by the potential transient blockade of the mu receptor could lead to improvement in the quality of life in chronic pain patients7. It is our position that LDN, by reducing the total inflammatory burden in the central nervous system and transiently blocking the mu-receptor, has a unique niche in the treatment of chronic pain. The recent advancements with naltrexone has led to it being employed more frequently in unique ways with success. We believe that LDN, as an adjunct to other non-opiate pain modalities, is a valuable addition to a multifaceted pain management approach. It is an inexpensive medication with relatively few side effects and provides a similar effect profile to the current standard of practice. It is clear from our case that LDN warrants further investigation as a treatment modality for chronic pain conditions. A retrospective cohort study has been initiated at Naval Medical Center Portsmouth, VA, in order to more accurately elucidate the effectiveness of LDN at reducing pain scores and daily opiate consumption in the active duty military population. The views expressed in this article are those of the author(s) and do not reflect the official policy or position of the Department of the Navy, Department of Defense, or the United States Government. I am a military service member. This work was prepared as part of my official duties. Title 17 U.S.C. 105 provides that 'Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government. Title 17 U.S.C. 101 defines a United States Government work as a work prepared by a military service member or employee of the United States Government as part of that person's official duties. References: 1. Center for Disease Control and Prevention. (April 3, 2018). Fibromyalgia. https://www.cdc.gov/arthritis/basics/fibromyalgia.htm 2. Raknes G, Småbrekke L. Low-dose naltrexone and opioid consumption: a drug utilization cohort study based on data from the Norwegian prescription database. Pharmacoepidemiol Drug Saf. 2017 Jun;26(6):685-693. 3. Younger J, Parkitny L, McLain D. The use of low-dose naltrexone (LDN) as a novel anti-inflammatory treatment for chronic pain. Clin Rheumatol. 2014 Apr;33(4):451-9. 4. Parkitny L, Younger J. Reduced Pro-Inflammatory Cytokines after Eight Weeks of Low-Dose Naltrexone for Fibromyalgia. Biomedicines. 2017 Apr 18;5(2). 5. Dantzer R (2007) Twenty years of research on cytokine- induced sickness behavior Brain, behavior, and immunity 6. Brown N, Panksepp J (2009) Low-dose naltrexone for disease prevention and quality of life. Med Hypotheses 72(3):333–337 7. Tempel A, Gardner EL, Zukin RS (1985) Neurochemical and functional correlates of naltrexone-induced opiate receptor up-regulation. 8. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 232(2):439–444 42. Zagon IS, McLaughlin PJ (1995) Gene-peptide relationships in the developing rat brain: the response of preproenkephalin mRNA and [Met5]-enkephalin to acute opioid antagonist (naltrexone) exposure. Brain Res Mol Brain Res 33(1):111–120 9. Wieseler-Frank J, Maier SF, Watkins LR (2005) Immune-to-brain communication dynamically modulates pain: physiological and pathological consequences. Brain Behav Immun 19(2):104–111
