N2  - Objective: To illustrate the use of robotic surgery in repairing a trans-mural isthmocele following treatment for a cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy. Design: A case report. Settings: A military medical center. Patients: 34 year old G4P1031 presented to the Emergency Department reporting acute onset of left lower quadrant abdominal pain. 18 months prior to her presentation, she was diagnosed with a cesarean section scar ectopic pregnancy and treated with intramuscular and intragestational sac methotrexate. Following treatment, she received depo Provera for 12 months and had resumed trying to conceive. Her hCG was negative. A transvaginal ultrasound was concerning for a full thickness defect in the anterior uterine wall which was confirmed with pelvic MRI. Interventions: Patient underwent combined diagnostic hysteroscopy followed by robotic assisted laparoscopic transmural isthmocele repair. Measurements/Results: Results to be presented. Conclusions: The incidence of cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy correlates with the rate of cesarean sections. The literature describes methotrexate as a safe and effective treatment; however, transmural isthmocele has not been previously described as a complication following treatment of a cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy. When future fertility is desired, a robot-assisted repair of the defect is a viable option.
