N2  - Objective: Our objective is to demonstrate a laparoscopic approach of managing a cesarean section scar residual ectopic pregnancy and laparoscopic suturing of the niche defect. Design: Intra-operative edited video. Settings: The gynaecology department at the University hospital of Wales, Cardiff. Patients: A 26 year old Para 1 + 1 (LSCS) presents with symptoms of irregular bleeding and pelvic pain. Three months previously, she had undergone a surgical termination of pregnancy followed by a repeat evacuation of retained products of conception. A pregnancy test was negative and transvaginal ultrasound scan detected a niche. Interventions: The patient was counselled and proceeded with a hysteroscopy, laparoscopy and repair of niche. Laparoscopic repair of the defect was performed. The Utero-vesical fold was reflected and the defect identified. The niche appeared to have residual pregnancy tissue within it which was removed. The defect was sutured with 0 Vicryl interrupted sutures. The patient made a good postoperative recovery and the symptoms improved. Measurements/Results: Conclusions: Laparoscopic niche repair is effective in selective cases. Both hysteroscopic and laparoscopic approaches to niche repair appear to be safe. Early detection and recognition of cesarean scar ectopic pregnancy is paramount with the use of transvaginal scanning to assist the diagnosis.
