N2  - Objective: To describe tips and tricks for laparoscopic resection of challenging cesarean scar pregnancies. Design: Laparoscopic surgical videos. Settings: The surgeries were performed at a large, safety net hospital. Patients: Two patients with cesarean scar pregnancies underwent surgical management via laparoscopic resection of the pregnancy. Upon entry into the abdominal cavity, the cesarean scar pregnancy was not readily identifiable and various techniques were used in order to complete the surgery with minimal blood loss. Interventions: Both patients with diagnosed cesarean scar pregnancies elected for management with laparoscopic resection of the pregnancy. Measurements/Results: In order to identify the cesarean scar pregnancy it was critical to restore normal anatomy. This was achieved by careful lysis of adhesions to allow for adequate visualization of the anterior lower uterine segment and cervix. A colpotomizer cup served as a landmark to aid in identification of the cesarean scar. Concomitant hysteroscopy was similarly helpful in locating the best location for hysterotomy. Cesarean scar pregnancies confer a larger risk of hemorrhage due to early myometrial invasion. Thus, minimizing blood loss is extremely important. Injecting vasopressin at the site of the cesarean scar pregnancy and using hemoclips on the uterine arteries to temporarily reduce blood flow to the uterus were techniques used to minimize blood loss. The isthmocele was repaired, after the pregnancy was removed, using v-loc suture. Conclusions: When the cesarean scar pregnancy is not easily identified laparoscopically, there are a number of techniques that can be used to aid in identification of the pregnancy while minimizing blood loss throughout surgery.
