N2  - Only recently we became aware of long term sequelae after a caesarean section in association with niches (uterine caesarean scar defects). These include postmenstrual spotting, dysmenorrhea, abnormal placentation and possibly also subfertility. In case of symptomatic large niches a laparoscopic niche resection can be considered. We studied safety, symptoms, ultrasound findings, Quality of life (QOL) and reproductive outcomes. Design: Prospective cohort study with consecutive inclusion among 180 symptomatic women with a large niche (residual myometrium (RM) <3 mm). Methods: Women completed questionnaires, validated menstrual score chart and received a vaginal ultrasound at baseline, six and 12 months after laparoscopic niche resection. Results: Our preliminary results show reduction in symptoms (80%) with a very strong reduction in postmenstrual spotting and dysmenorrhea. 6 out of 8 QOL subdomains of the validated SF-36 showed a significant and relevant improvement. The RM increased statistically significant at follow-up. The intrauterine fluid was resolved in almost 90% of the women with intra-uterine fluid at baseline. Sofar we evaluated pregnancy outcomes in the first 101 women, 61% of the women conceived with a median time to pregnancy of 3 months after discontinuation of contraceptives, while almost half of these women had previous failed IVF procedures. Conclusions: A laparoscopic niche resection is a safe procedure and reduces postmenstrual spotting, dysmenorrhea, intrauterine fluid, increased the RM, improves the QOL and results in high pregnancy rates. However comparative studies with other therapies or expectant management is needed to establish the additional value concerning reproductive outcomes.
