N2  - OBJECTIVE: UFs arise from the genetic transformation of a single myometrial (MM) stem cell (SC) into a tumor-initiating cell (UF-SC) that seeds and sustains fibroid growth through asymmetric cell divisions and monoclonal expansion. Heretofore, the genetic drivers dominantly responsible for SC transformation have been identified. The most prevalent among these, are somatic mutations in the MED12. However, the molecular basis by which MED12 mutations drive UF formation are unknown. In addition, the crosstalk between UF-SC and their supportive niche is not well understood. Here, we interrogated reciprocal signaling between UF-SCs and their differentiated cell (UF-DC) progeny. DESIGN: Research study on the cross-talk of Med12 mutation and inflammation in uterine fibroid pathogenesis MATERIALS AND METHODS: We isolated MED12-mutant UF-SCs based on CD44 and Stro-1 cell surface markers (CD44+Stro-1+) and matched UF-DC (CD44-Stro-1-) both sorted by flow cytometry and we subjected them to RNAseq analysis. To elucidate the impact of mutant Med12 on cytokine dysregulation, we performed gain of function to mimic Med12-mutant UFSCs using CDK8/19 kinase Mediator kinase inhibition by CCT251545 (CCT). The potential regulated targets (PD-L1, PD-1, TNF-α, IL-8, IL-1α and NF-kB) expression were validated by flow cytometry analysis. The generated data was supported by immunohistochemistry analysis. Statistical analysis was done using paired t-test. Values were considered statistically significant when P was less than 0.05. RESULTS: RNAseq data on human UF samples, show elevated expression levels of pro-inflammatory markers observed in MED12-mutant UF SCs persisted during tumor differentiation, leading to incipient lesions characterized by massive inflammation based on significant increase in the expression of TNF-α, IL-1α and NF-kB in UF patient samples versus matched controls (P < 0.005). This results was validated by immunohistochemistry analysis. In addition, we found also, a significant association of macrophages infiltration in UF compared to adjacent myometrium (P < 0.005). Furthermore, our result show that ectopic expression in uterine smooth muscle cells of mutant, but not WT, MED12 triggered reduced Mediator kinase activity and enhanced expression of PD-L1. Thus, like pro-inflammatory cytokines, PD-L1 is an apparent target of direct and acute regulation by MED12. The treatment of MM SCs with mediator kinase inhibitors confirmed enhanced expression of PD-L1, and, strikingly, we also observed persistent upregulation of PD-L1 in MED12mutant UFs compared to autologous MM. CONCLUSIONS: our data demonstrate that UF-DC cooperate with UFSC through PD-L1/PD-1 interaction and NF-Kb- dependent inflammatory cytokines to promote UF tumor growth.
