N2  - INTRODUCTION: Uterine fibroids, benign smooth muscle tumors of the myometrium, occur in up to 70% of women, making them the most common tumors in women and the leading indication for hysterectomies due to associated morbidities. Despite the high prevalence of uterine fibroids and the significant healthcare burden they pose, very little is known about the etiology and pathogenesis of the disease. We hypothesize that fibroids arise from myometrial stem/progenitor cells when mutated or dysregulated. Mesenchymal stem/progenitor cells have been identified in the endometrial stroma by co-expression of CD146 and CD140b or by single marker expression of SUSD2. Because of the shared embryonic origins of the stroma and myometrium, we hypothesize that SUSD2 and/or CD146/CD140b co-expression will also enrich for myometrial and fibroid stem/progenitor cells. METHODS: Human normal myometrial (Myo) and fibroid (Fib) specimens were used for localization of CD146+CD140b+ and SUSD2+ cells in tissue sections and for isolation by flow cytometry and magnetic beads, respectively. Stem cell activity of CD146+CD140b+ and SUSD2+ cells was assessed by colony formation, differentiation into various mesenchymal lineages and xenotransplantation. RESULTS: Both CD146+CD140b+ and SUSD2+ cells are almost exclusively localized to the vasculature in Myo and Fib tissues similar to endometrial stroma (ST), which is a proposed mesenchymal stem cell niche. Significantly fewer CD146+CD140b+ (P=0.03) and SUSD2+ (P=0.003) cells were present in Fib compared to matched Myo, and did not differ by stage of the menstrual cycle. CD146+CD140b+ (P=0.007) and SUSD2+(P<0.0001) cells were enriched for colony forming units (CFUs) over control cells in Myo. SUSD2+ cells from matched Myo and Fib cells were further analyzed for colony formation under normoxic versus hypoxic conditions, which showed that although there was a similar percentage of CFUs, it took significantly longer (P=0.008, Myo; P=0.02, Fib) for colonies to form in normoxia. SUSD2+ cells undergo adipogenic and osteogenic differentiation and preliminary results suggest that SUSD2+ cells form tumors when transplanted under the renal capsule of immunocompromised mice. CONCLUSION: Our results suggest that the endometrial ST stem/progenitor cell markers CD146/CD140b and SUSD2 enrich for Myo and Fib cells with stem/progenitor activity. On going experiments include 1) RNA-seq to compare mesenchymal stem/progenitor cells from ST, Myo and Fib, and 2) testing the potential of Myo stem/progenitor cells to contribute to Fib formation when mutated or dysregulated. Results from these studies will provide important insight into the etiology and pathogenesis of uterine fibroids and may lead to new therapeutic tools to better manage the disease.
