N2  - OBJECTIVE: Metagenomics was developed as a means to detect and classify bacteria, knowing that <10% of all bacteria are culturable. Here we sought to visualize, localize, and estimate the abundance of intact microbiota in term and preterm gestations using a signal amplified 16S universal in situ hybridization (ISH) probe set for bacterial rRNA, alongside traditional histologic methods of Warthin-Starry and Gram stains and clinical microbiology culture. STUDY DESIGN: Placental tissue was sterile dissected from subjects experiencing a spontaneous PTB with (n 10) and without (n 9) preterm premature rupture of membranes (PPROM), as well as medically indicated PTB (n 10) and term (n 26) “sterile” Cesarean delivered pregnancies. Placental microbiota were visualized and enumerated using an in situ hybridization probe set designed against highly conserved regions of the bacterial 16S ribosome, which produces an amplified stable signal using branched DNA probes. Extracted bacterial nucleic acid fromthese same samples were subjected to 16S rRNA gene metagenomic sequencing (Illumina, V4) for taxonomical analysis, and samples were sent immediately at delivery for aerobic and anaerobic culture in our clinical labs. RESULTS: Molecular ISH of bacterial rRNA enabled visualization and localization of microbiota after systematic high power scanning (Fig.1A). Despite the absence of inflammation, bacterial clusters were localized (in descending order) to the villus parenchyma and/or syncytiotrophoblast, the maternal intervillous space, and the chorion (Fig. 1B). Metagenomic 16S rRNA gene sequencing revealed 132 operational taxonomical units not found in kit negative (n 3) or gnotobiotic (germ-free) mouse placentae (n 3) controls (Fig.1C). Of particular interest, despite both direct rRNA visualization and metagenomic evidence of bacteria, clinical cultures were never positive (Table). CONCLUSION: Intact placental bacteria are not abundant nor culturable by standard clinical techniques, but are observed within the placenta in all term and preterm pregnancies examined, and with a taxonomical makeup distinct from germ-free controls. These findings are consistent with metagenomics analysis characterizing the placental microbiome as a unique low-biomass niche. This report serves as first line morphologic evidence for a low biomass microbial communities in the placental villous tree. (Figure Presented).
