N2  - Objectives: To describe the surgical repair of a dehiscence/fistulous tract between the cervical canal and the anterior uterine surface following a previous VBAC as well as the following uneventful pregnancy and cesarean delivery (CD) of a near term neonate. Methods: A 27 y.o. G4P2012 female was admitted at 36w for scheduled CD, possible hysterectomy for suspected placenta increta/percreta by MRI. The US diagnosis was only a focal placenta accreta near the cervix. Past OB history is significant for a previous CD for macrosomia followed by a successful VBAC after which a uterine “window” on manual exploration of the uterus was noted. A later saline infusion sonography (SIS) showed a niche and a fistula formation between the cervical canal and the anterior uterine surface. Seven months allowed for spontaneous resolution of the fistula, revealed the same finding on SIS. Robotic-assisted repair of the uterine defect was performed. Results: In the present pregnancy, at 16 weeks, placenta previa and a slight suspicion of a focal placenta accreta was noted. At 34 weeks the findings were corroborated by MRI and she underwent a classical cesarean section delivering a male infant with APGAR of 8/9, 3260 g. Most of the placenta separated easily with a small difficulty at its lower edge. Estimated blood loss was 900cc. Postoperative course was uncomplicated. Placental pathology reveled focal villous edema. Conclusions: A total of 60 repairs were performed by different surgical methods (laparoscopy, laparotomy). Only one was done by robotic assisted surgery. There were 12 pregnancies (20%) with successful deliveries of live newborns (18%). Due to the low numbers, it is impossible to evaluate the success rate of the three surgical techniques. Whether surgical repair of post CD niches/dehiscences is needed at all is not clear, however there is no doubt about the justified repair of fistulas. Our case qualifies for the latter indication. A detailed account of the above cases in the literature will be presented.
