N2  - INTRODUCTION: Uterine fibroids, which are benign smooth muscle tumors of the myometrium, are estimated to occur in up to 70% of women, making them the most common tumors in women and the leading cause of hysterectomies because of associated morbidities. Despite their prevalence and significant health care burden, little is known about their etiology. Myometrial stem cells participate in growth/expansion and repair of the tissue during the menstrual cycle, pregnancy and postpartum uterine involution. We hypothesize that fibroids arise from these myometrial stem/progenitor cells when dysregulated. Putative mesenchymal stem/ progenitor cells have been identified in the stroma of the uterus by coexpression of CD146 and CD140b and we hypothesize that these markers will also enrich for myometrial stem/progenitor cells. METHODS: Human normal myometrial and fibroid specimens were used for localization of CD146 and CD140b co-expressing cells in tissue sections and for isolation of co-expressing cells by flow cytometry. In vitro stem cell activity of CD146+CD140b+ cells was assessed by colony formation and differentiation into various mesenchymal lineages under defined culture conditions. RESULTS: Preliminary results indicate that CD146+CD140b+ myometrial cells undergo adipogenic, osteogenic and chondrogenic differentiation and have greater colony forming potential by forming more total colonies and more alkaline phosphatase positive colonies compared to control cells, all hallmarks of mesenchymal stem cells. Also, cells co-expressing CD146 and CD140b are located perivascularly, a proposed mesenchymal stem cell niche. A greater percentage of CD146+CD140b+ cells was observed in the myometria of premenopausal compared to postmenopausal women. The percentage of CD146+CD140b+ cells in fibroids compared to normal myometrium varies, however our data suggests a correlation between low cellular content and low percentage of CD146+CD140b+ cells in fibroids. CONCLUSIONS: Our results suggest that CD146 and CD140b enrich for myometrial and stem/progenitor cells. Future experiments will test the in vivo stem cell activity of CD146+CD140b+ cells by xenotransplantation and determine the potential of CD146+CD140b+ myometrial cells to contribute to fibroid formation when mutated or dysregulated. Results from these and future studies will provide important insight into the etiology and pathogenesis of uterine fibroids and may lead to new therapeutic tools to better manage the disease.
