N2  - Introduction: Isthmocele of the uterine scar is an underdiagnosed consequence of cesarean sections. It causes abnormal uterine bleeding, pain or infertility. Treatment options are limited. We present the operative and follow up data of a laparoscopic excision of the scar in rendezvous technique. Methods: The internal database of the university hospital of Berne was searched for patients operated on isthmocele from 2008-2014. Patients were contacted in person for follow-up. 31 patients were operated (mean age: 34,1, mean BMI: 24,6), follow up was obtained in 30. Results: Patients were operated by laparoscopic excision of the fibrotic tissue with simultaneous hysteroscopy demonstrating the dehiscence with translumination (mean operating time: 130 min, mean blood loss: 42 ml, no intraoperative complications). Mean follow-up time was 2,1 years. Bleeding disorders and pain were the indication for surgery in 13 patients. In 9 patients (69%) the symptoms improved significantly afterwards. 26 patients were operated for planned pregnancy. Of these 6 have not tried to get pregnant until know. Of the 20 patients left, 5 had a history of infertility before the first pregnancy. Eleven (55%) became pregnant, two had early abortions, one a cesarean scar pregnancy. Two are currently pregnant. 6 delivered a term pregnancy. Conclusions: The laparoscopic approach in rendezvous technique for isthmocele is a feasible, low risk operation. The outcome for uterine bleeding disorders and pain is good. For treatment of secondary infertility the operative correction of the isthmocele could help achieve a pregnancy in over half the patients, this probably being only one component of the reason of infertility.
