N2  - Cesarean section scar ectopic pregnancies are rare, but in patients with a history of one cesarean section they represent 6% of all ectopic pregnancies. The first laparoscopic removal was reported in 1999. Surgical management is usually preferred over medical management due to higher rates of success, faster recovery to negative beta-HCG levels and decreased complication rates. Most reports recommend removing the ectopic pregnancy and repairing any defect. In our technique, we also remove any abnormal surrounding isthmocele tissue in order to reapproximate normal tissue edges. This technique leads to improved outcomes, decreased recurrence rates and decreased adhesion formation. In this video, we present a case of a laparoscopic resection of cesarean section scar ectopic pregnancy with isthmocele repair. Our patient underwent an uncomplicated surgery followed by appropriately decreasing beta-HCG levels and a repeat sonohysterogram that showed no residual cesarean section scar defect.
