N2  - Study Objective: Specify basic echographic and hysteroscopic criteria of partly faulty uterus scar, evaluate their informativeness on the stage of pregnancy planning. Evaluate clinical significance of office hysteroscopy in estimation of indications to metroplasty. Design: Retrospective study. Setting: Gynecology department of the center, 2014. Patients: 16 patients with identified partly faulty uterus scar after 1 or more years after cesarean section on the stage of pregnancy planning. Following ultrasonic signs were selection criteria: niche-shape myometrium defect on the side of uterine cavity with unchanged myometrium thickness 4-5 mm or less; myometrium defect of serous tunic of uterus traction + “niche” . Additional criteria: absence of vascularization of “unchanged” myometrium, ligature, scar endometriosis. Intervention: Interventions: hydrosonography was performed on all the patients and office hysteroscopy. Hydrosonography allowed to evaluating the state of uterus surgical scar and determining real depth of myometrium defect and minimal uterine wall thickness of the area. As a result of hysteroscopy: 27% endometriosis of surgical scar, 36% old ligatures. Niches in the area of the scar were visualized in all patients, however, it was impossible to identify their real depth. Indications for surgery: thinning of uterus surgery scar with its endometriosis and foreign body; myometrium thinning in the scar area up to 3 mm or less.Indications for metroplasty-pregnancy planning. Measurements and Main Results: effectiveness of diagnostics is based on clinical, echographic, and endoscopic methods. Myometriumthinning in the uterus surgery scar area up to 3-4 mm together with space-occupying mass, absence of substantial myometrium vascularization, myometry at energy mapping is an indication to additional study methods: hydrosonography and hysteroscopy. If diagnose is confirmed metroplasty with subsequent 6 month rehabilitation is appropriate. Conclusion: Conclusions: For diagnosing faulty uterus scar on stage of pregnancy planning consecutive complex examination is required.
