N2  - Study question: Human and mouse endometrium undergo dramatic cellular changes during pregnancy and reorganization at postpartum. Somatic stem cells maintain homeostasis of the tissue by providing cell reservoir critical for regeneration 1. We hypothesized endometrial cells with quiescent properties (stem/progenitors) exist after uterine remodelling and are involve in the regeneration of endometrial tissue. Summary answer: A small population of stromal label-retaining cells (LRCs) in mouse endometriumwas identified at postpartum day 21 (11-week chased). These cells remained quiescent until after parturition when a significant portion of them underwent proliferation, suggesting their involvement in the regeneration of the mouse endometrium. What is known already: The well-established LRC technique was adapted for understanding the endometrial stem cell niche. Using a mouse model, quiescent cells (a property of somatic stem cells) retaining theDNAsynthesis label (bromodeoxyuridine, BrdU) after a prolong chase are considered to be somatic stem cells 2-3. Endometrial LRCs have been identified in cycling mice 4-5 and a menstruation breakdown mouse model 6. Study design, size, duration: Prepubertal C57BL/6J female mice were labelled with BrdU, followed by a chase period of up to11 weeks. Mice in remodelling group were mated and sacrificed at gestational day 7 and 14, and postpartum day 1, 3, 7, 14 and 21. Control mice were non-pregnant mice sacrificed at 11-week chased. Participants/materials, setting, methods: Four to five animals were used for each group. Uteri collected at various chase periods and fixed with4%paraformaldehyde. LRCs were identified and phenotyped by immunohistochemistry and immunofluorescence using an anti-BrdU antibody and cell specific markers. Main results and the role of chance: In non-pregnant mice (11-week chase), 0.96% stromal nuclei contained BrdU and considered as LRCs. During gestation, stromalLRCslocalized exclusively to the inter-implantation/placentation loci and remained quiescent (proliferative marker Ki-672). By postpartum day 1, 43.89% of the stromal LRCs underwent proliferation (Ki-67+). The percentage declined quickly thereafter, suggesting the cells are recruited into endometrial regeneration immediately after parturition. Hence lower number of stromal LRCs were observed from the remodelling group when compared with control (0.41% stromal LRCs in remodeling group, P < 0.05). These cells were primarily located beneath epithelium, adjacent to blood vessels and near endometrialmyometrial junction. Stromal LRCs at postpartum day 21 co-expressed with embryonic stem cell marker Sall4 (31.20%), mesenchymal stem cell markers: Sca-1 (72.04%), CD90 (45.26%), CD140b (44.51%), CD44 (23.72%), and CD146 (1.81%). Limitations, reason for caution: Our findings provide evidence that stromal LRCs participate in remodeling of mouse endometrium. However, LRCs in human endometrium in vivo cannot be conducted as BrdU can cause health hazards. Also, direct extrapolation of the observations in mouse to human has to be cautious as the structure of their endometrium is different. Wider implications of the findings: In agreementwith previous studies4-5, a subpopulation of candidate stem/progenitor cells was identified after uterine remodelling. The currentmethodmay be used to exploit the role of this population of cells in regulating endometrial regeneration and differentiation in its physiological state.
