N2  - Objectives: To survey the frequency of images of a niche at the site of the uterine scar after Cesarean section of single-layer interrupted suture. Materials: Thirty-two women undergoing Cesarean section in our hospital were involved in this observational prospective cohort study. Methods: Saline contrast sonohysterography (SCSH) was performed 3 to 4 months after Cesarean section. The thickness of the residual myometrium, the thickness of the myometrium bordering the scar and the depth of the filling defect in the scar (i.e. the 'niche', defined as a triangular, anechoic area at the presumed site of incision) were recorded in each case. Results: In 13/32 (40.6%) patients a niche with a depth of 3.7±2.1 (range, 0.8-7.2) mm was identified. In these patients the residual myometrium measured 6.0±2.5 (range, 2.3-11.9) mm vs. 10.4±2.8 (range, 6.6-18.1) mm in the remaining 19 patients without a niche. Conclusions: Niche can be identified by SCSH following a Cesarean section of single-layer interrupted hysterotomy closure in about 40% of patients. Other ways of myometrium closure procedure should be examined to look for better one.
