N2  - Study Objective: To describe an additional case of laparoscopic management of CSD while illustrating the documented progression of IST to CSD and stressing their clinical differences. Design: Case report. Setting: Private tertiary hospital in São Paulo, Brazil Patients: A 41-year-old woman, gesta 6, abortion 3, cesarean 3, with a previous diagnosis of IST that further progressed to a CSD during a 2- year period of follow-up. Intervention: Laparoscopic identification and repair of a CSD with three separate sutures of polidioxanone 2-0, with the aid of intraoperative hysteroscopy and dye-injection into the CSD pseudosac. Measurements and Main Results: Final diagnosis was established exclusively by means of transvaginal ultrasound, aided by liquid already present inside the pseudosac. (Figure presented) Despite a large CSD with a pseudosac of 4.8 x 3.9 x 1.7 cm, the patient was not operated until one year later, when symptoms of suprapubic pain first developed and the pseudosac grew to 6.8 x 6.2 x 4.7 cm (volume 103 cc3). (Figure presented) The patient was using a levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine device (IUD) during the whole period, and abnormal bleeding did not develop. The procedure was performed exclusively by laparoscopy and lasted 170 minutes. Bleeding was 10 ml and the patient was discharged after 48 hours in good conditions. The IUD was found topic and removed at the end of the procedure. The first month of follow-up after surgery was uneventful. Six-month follow-up will be presented during the meeting. This case represents an additional case to those 3 other ones reported of laparoscopic treatment of CSD. Conclusion: Asymptomatic progression of IST to CSD is possible, especially in patients using a levenorgestrel-releasing IUD. Even the presence of large CSD may be asymptomatic. Laparoscopic management of a CSD is feasible and provides short-term benefits.
