N2  - Problem: Endometriosis is characterized by the growth of endometrial tissue that normally lines the uterine cavity, outside of the uterus. Endometriosis is estimated to occur in 7-10% of women. Angiogenesis appears to be one of the crucial processes involved in its pathogenesis and hence anti-angiogenesis therapy provides a potential therapeutic option. Endothelial progenitor cells (EPCs) derivedfrom bone marrow have been documented to aid neoangiogenesis. Cytokines such as SDF-1 have a potent chemotactic effect on EPCs and have been shown to recruit and retain EPCs to the neoangiogenic sites. We hypothesize that elevated SDF-1 in endometriosis patients supports the mobilization and retention of EPCs to the neoangiogenesis niche. Method of study: Twenty endometriosis and six control samples were obtained from Kingston General Hospital. Immunohistochemistry was performedto localize SDF-1 and CD34+ progenitor cells using human anti-SDF-1 and human anti-CD34 antibodies. Co-localization of SDF-1 and CD34 was performed using suitable fluorochrome tagged secondary antibodies. Results: We have detected increased expression of SDF-1 in endometriosis samples compared with controls. To identify a correlation between SDF-1 and EPC recruitment, we performed CD34 immunostaining and observed overabundance of CD34+ progenitorcells in patients with endometriosis. CD34+ progenitor cells were found to be associated with newly developing vasculature. Colocalization of SDF-1 and CD34 further confirmed their abundance in and around newly developed vessels. Conclusion: This data strongly suggests that SDF-1 may be responsible for CD34+ progenitor cell recruitment and retention to the neoangiogenic sites in endometriosis. Studies blocking SDF-1 in combination with other anti-angiogenic agents in a mouse model of endometriosis are in progress.
